window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"math-olympiad/index.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Math Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IMO","x":"Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and are strong at math. Target: national team selection, then IMO-level problem solving."},{"u":"math-olympiad/index.html#read-first-eligibility-and-timing","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and timing","x":"- The IMO generally requires contestants to be under 20 on the day of the contest and not enrolled at a university. As a high-school senior, this cycle is probably your last chance. Verify the rules with your country's olympiad committee. - National selection often starts months before the IMO (July). Find your country's dates now and plan backwards from them. - The IMO paper: 2 days × 3 problems × 4.5 hours, 7 points each. Problems cover Algebra, Combinatorics, Geometry, and Number theory, ordered roughly easy → hard (P1/P4 easiest, P3/P6 hardest)."},{"u":"math-olympiad/index.html#the-map","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"The map","x":"Module Time You can… 01 Problem solving & proof writing 2–3 wk write rigorous proofs and use the core strategies 02 Algebra 4–6 wk inequalities, polynomials, functional equations 03 Number theory 4–6 wk modular arithmetic, orders, LTE, Diophantine equations 04 Combinatorics 5–7 wk counting, invariants, extremal arguments, graphs, games 05 Geometry 5–7 wk angle chasing → power of a point → projective/inversion → bash methods 06 Contest practice & solution writing ongoing sit full mocks and write solutions that earn full marks Modules 02–05 run in parallel: rotate topics weekly instead of finish"},{"u":"math-olympiad/index.html#interactive-tools-interactive","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Interactive tools → interactive/","x":"- Triangle centers explorer: drag a triangle and see the circumcenter, incenter, centroid, orthocenter, Euler line, and nine-point circle live. - Modular arithmetic lab: multiplication tables mod n, orders, primitive roots, Fermat/Euler check. - Flashcards: spaced repetition for theorems, lemmas, and techniques."},{"u":"math-olympiad/index.html#weekly-routine-1012-h","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","x":"Day Do 3 days Topic study: read one handout section, then solve 4–6 problems from it 1 day Hard problem: one problem above your level, 1.5–2 h, no hints for the first hour 1 day Mock: 3 problems in 4.5 h (from week 3), then grade yourself strictly 1 day Review: write up solutions cleanly, read official solutions, update progress.md The golden rule: stay stuck for at least 30–60 min before looking at a hint. When you read a solution, write down the key idea in one line. That line is what you'll remember and reuse. Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","x":"Goal: have a reliable process for attacking unfamiliar problems, and write proofs a grader can't fault. · Time: 2–3 weeks"},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html#skip-test","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Prove by strong induction that every integer ≥ 2 is a product of primes. - [ ] Prove \\sqrt 2 is irrational by contradiction, and \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3} too. - [ ] Use pigeonhole: among any 5 points in a unit square, two are within \\tfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}. - [ ] Find an invariant: can a chessboard with two opposite corners removed be tiled by dominoes? - [ ] Use the extremal principle: show a finite set of points, not all collinear, has a line through exactly 2 of them (Sylvester–Gallai)."},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html#core-ideas","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Process (Pólya): understand → try small cases → find a pattern/conjecture → prove → check. - Small cases first. Compute n=1,2,3,4,5. Most olympiad insights come from data. - Work backwards from what's needed. Draw a big, accurate figure (geometry and combinatorics). - Proof toolbox: - Direct, contrapositive, contradiction. - Induction: weak, strong, and \"strengthen the hypothesis\" (proving something stronger is often easier). - Pigeonhole: n+1 objects in n boxes; more generally, some box has \\ge\\lceil N/k\\rceil. - Extremal principle: look at the largest/smallest/first object, then derive a c"},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html#learn-from","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ The Art and Craft of Problem Solving, Paul Zeitz 💲: ch. 1–3 (strategies, tactics). The best first book. - Problem-Solving Strategies, Arthur Engel 💲: ch. 1–4 (invariants, coloring, extremal, pigeonhole). A huge problem bank. - Evan Chen: Notes on proof writing: see his handouts page (\"Proof writing\" and the \"English\" handout). - How to Write Proofs, Larry Cusick (short guide)."},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html#build","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"Build","x":"- Write 10 complete proofs of Engel ch. 1–4 problems. Have someone (or your future self, a week later) grade them strictly from 0–7."},{"u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html#traps","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","h":"Traps","x":"- \"Proof by example\": checking cases is not a proof. - Skipping writing practice. At IMO level, ~1/3 of lost points are from incomplete write-ups, not wrong ideas."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"02 · Algebra","x":"Goal: inequalities, polynomials, functional equations, and sequences at national-to-IMO level. · Time: 4–6 weeks (in rotation)"},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#skip-test","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Prove AM-GM for n variables, and use it to minimize x + \\frac{4}{x^2} for x0. - [ ] Prove \\frac{a}{b+c}+\\frac{b}{c+a}+\\frac{c}{a+b}\\ge\\frac32 (Nesbitt) in two different ways. - [ ] Find all f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R with f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y) and f monotonic. - [ ] Use Vieta: if x^3-6x^2+11x-6 has roots r,s,t, find r^2+s^2+t^2 without solving. - [ ] Explain when equality holds in Cauchy–Schwarz."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#core-ideas","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Core ideas","x":""},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#inequalities","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Inequalities","x":"- AM-GM: \\frac{x1+\\dots+xn}{n}\\ge\\sqrt[n]{x1\\cdots xn}, with equality iff all are equal. Weighted version too. - Power mean chain: QM ≥ AM ≥ GM ≥ HM. - Cauchy–Schwarz: \\left(\\sum ai^2\\right)\\left(\\sum bi^2\\right)\\ge\\left(\\sum aibi\\right)^2. Titu/Engel form: \\sum\\frac{ai^2}{bi}\\ge\\frac{(\\sum ai)^2}{\\sum bi}. - Hölder, rearrangement, Chebyshev, Schur (t0: \\sum a^t(a-b)(a-c)\\ge0), Muirhead (for symmetric polynomial inequalities). - Jensen: for convex f, f\\!\\left(\\frac{\\sum xi}{n}\\right)\\le\\frac{\\sum f(xi)}{n}. Tangent line trick for \\sum f(xi) with \\sum xi fixed. - Techniques: homogenize, normali"},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#polynomials","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Polynomials","x":"- Factor theorem, Vieta's formulas, Rational root theorem. - Integer-coefficient trick: a-b \\mid P(a)-P(b). - Roots of unity filter: \\frac1n\\sum{k=0}^{n-1}P(\\omega^k) extracts the coefficients with index ≡ 0 (mod n). - Lagrange interpolation, finite differences (a degree-d polynomial has a constant d-th difference). - Irreducibility: Eisenstein's criterion; the mod-p reduction trick."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#functional-equations","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Functional equations","x":"- Standard plays: substitute x=y=0, y=0, y=x, y=-x, x=1. Find f(0) and f(1). - Prove injective / surjective, find fixed points, use symmetry (swap x,y). - Cauchy's equation f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y): over \\mathbb Q gives f(x)=cx. Over \\mathbb R it needs extra conditions (monotonic, bounded on an interval, or continuous). - Guess the answer (usually f(x)=x, cx, x^2, constant), verify it, then prove it's the only one. - Over \\mathbb N / \\mathbb Z: induction, bounding, and number theory."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#sequences-sums","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Sequences & sums","x":"- Linear recurrences: characteristic equation. Telescoping. Bounding by monotonicity/convergence."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#learn-from","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Evan Chen: \"A Brief Introduction to Olympiad Inequalities\", plus his functional equations material on the handouts page. - ⭐ Yufei Zhao's olympiad handouts: inequalities, polynomials, and more. - Secrets in Inequalities, Pham Kim Hung 💲 · Functional Equations, Venkatachala 💲 · Problems from the Book, Andreescu & Dospinescu 💲 (advanced). - AoPS: Intermediate Algebra 💲 if you need a gentler ramp."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#build","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Build","x":"- Solve the A1–A4 problems from the last 5 years of IMO Shortlists. Make a one-line \"key idea\" note for each."},{"u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html#traps","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 02 · Algebra","h":"Traps","x":"- Applying AM-GM where the equality case can't happen. - In functional equations, forgetting to check the solution in the original equation, and forgetting the pathological solutions of Cauchy."},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"03 · Number Theory","x":"Goal: divisibility, modular arithmetic, orders, and Diophantine equations at IMO level. · Time: 4–6 weeks (in rotation) 👉 Use the Modular arithmetic lab to see orders and primitive roots."},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html#skip-test","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Prove there are infinitely many primes ≡ 3 (mod 4). - [ ] Use Fermat's little theorem to find 2^{100}\\bmod 13. - [ ] Show that if \\text{ord}p(a)=d then d\\mid p-1. - [ ] Use LTE to find v2(3^{2^{n}}-1). - [ ] Solve x^2-2y^2=1 in positive integers (Pell) and describe all solutions. - [ ] Find all n with n \\mid 2^n - 1."},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html#core-ideas","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Divisibility & gcd: Euclid's algorithm, Bézout (ax+by=\\gcd(a,b)), \\gcd(a^m-1,a^n-1)=a^{\\gcd(m,n)}-1. - Fundamental theorem of arithmetic. p-adic valuation vp(n): think of numbers as exponent vectors. - Modular arithmetic: inverses exist iff \\gcd(a,n)=1. CRT: you can solve congruences modulo coprime moduli independently. - Fermat: a^{p-1}\\equiv1\\pmod p. Euler: a^{\\varphi(n)}\\equiv1\\pmod n. Wilson: (p-1)!\\equiv-1\\pmod p. - Order: \\text{ord}n(a) = the smallest d with a^d\\equiv1. Key lemma: a^k\\equiv1 \\iff \\text{ord}\\mid k. This is the most-used idea in olympiad NT. - Primitive roots exist mod 1"},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html#learn-from","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Modern Olympiad Number Theory, Aditya Khurmi: a free PDF shared by the author on AoPS (search the title). Thorough and olympiad-focused. - Evan Chen's NT handouts (orders) · Yufei Zhao: NT handouts. - Number Theory: Structures, Examples, and Problems, Andreescu & Andrica 💲 · 104 Number Theory Problems, Andreescu et al. 💲. - AoPS: Introduction to Number Theory 💲 if you need the basics."},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html#build","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"Build","x":"- Use the lab to find the order of every a mod 13, then prove the pattern you see (the orders divide 12; there are exactly \\varphi(d) elements of order d). - Solve N1–N4 from recent IMO Shortlists."},{"u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html#traps","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 03 · Number Theory","h":"Traps","x":"- Dividing in congruences without checking that the inverse exists. - Forgetting the p=2 special case of LTE and quadratic residues."},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"04 · Combinatorics","x":"Goal: the hardest and least formulaic IMO area. Build a toolkit, then lots of experience. · Time: 5–7 weeks (in rotation)"},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html#skip-test","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Count lattice paths from (0,0) to (m,n) and give a bijective proof of \\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n}{n-k}. - [ ] Use stars and bars: the number of solutions of x1+\\dots+xk=n with xi\\ge0. - [ ] Inclusion–exclusion: count derangements Dn. - [ ] Double counting: prove \\sumv \\deg v = 2 E , and \\sumk k\\binom nk = n2^{n-1}. - [ ] Game theory: find the winning positions in Nim. - [ ] Prove R(3,3)=6."},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html#core-ideas","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Counting: product/sum rule, permutations, \\binom nk, stars and bars, inclusion–exclusion, bijections (count something else that is easier). - Double counting: count the same set two ways, e.g. pairs (person, club) by person and by club. - Recursion: set up an in terms of smaller cases (tilings, paths). Catalan numbers Cn=\\frac1{n+1}\\binom{2n}{n}. - Generating functions: \\sum anx^n turns counting into algebra. - Pigeonhole, extremal, invariants, monovariants, coloring (from module 01) are the combinatorics weapons. - Graph theory: degrees, trees ( E = V -1), bipartite ⟺ no odd cycle, Euler pa"},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html#learn-from","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Problem Solving Methods in Combinatorics, Pablo Soberón 💲: short, by technique, IMO-focused. - Yufei Zhao's combinatorics handouts (e.g. \"Combinatorics: a few techniques\", double counting). - 102 Combinatorial Problems, Andreescu & Feng 💲 · Engel ch. on combinatorics · Evan Chen's handouts. - AoPS: Intro / Intermediate Counting & Probability 💲 for the basics."},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html#build","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"Build","x":"- Solve C1–C4 from the last 5–8 IMO Shortlists. Combinatorics gets better mostly with volume, so aim for 60+ problems."},{"u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html#traps","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 04 · Combinatorics","h":"Traps","x":"- Overcounting from treating identical objects as distinct (or the reverse). - For max/min problems: giving only the construction or only the bound."},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"05 · Geometry","x":"Goal: from angle chasing to projective tools, plus reliable computational backups. · Time: 5–7 weeks (in rotation) 👉 Use the Triangle centers explorer while studying this module."},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html#skip-test","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Prove the four points of a cyclic quadrilateral satisfy \"opposite angles sum to 180°\", and the converse. - [ ] State and use the power of a point and the radical axis theorem. - [ ] Prove the orthocenter's reflection over a side lies on the circumcircle. - [ ] Prove the incenter–excenter lemma: I, B, C, IA all lie on a circle centered at the arc midpoint MA. - [ ] Explain the Euler line and the nine-point circle (center, radius R/2). - [ ] Set up a problem in complex numbers with the unit circle as the circumcircle."},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html#core-ideas-in-learning-order","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"Core ideas (in learning order)","x":"1. Angle chasing: inscribed angle theorem, cyclic quadrilaterals, tangent–chord angle. Directed angles mod 180° avoid configuration issues. 2. Triangle centers & lemmas: centroid G, circumcenter O, incenter I, orthocenter H. Euler line O,G,H with OG:GH=1:2. Nine-point circle. Incenter–excenter lemma. Reflections of H. 3. Length tools: law of sines/cosines, Ceva and Menelaus (with the trig version), Stewart, Ptolemy. 4. Circles: power of a point, radical axis / radical center, coaxal circles, tangent circles. 5. Transformations: homothety (e.g. the incircle–excircle homothety), spiral similarit"},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html#learn-from","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Euclidean Geometry in Mathematical Olympiads (EGMO), Evan Chen 💲. The olympiad geometry book. Part I (ch. 1–4) is essential, Part II for IMO-level. Some chapters are free on his website. - Yufei Zhao: Lemmas in Euclidean geometry, Cyclic quadrilaterals handouts. - Lemmas in Olympiad Geometry, Andreescu, Korsky & Pohoata 💲 · Geometry Revisited, Coxeter & Greitzer 💲 (classic). - GeoGebra: draw every problem precisely. It's free."},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html#build","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"Build","x":"- For every geometry problem: (1) draw an accurate figure, (2) guess the key concurrency/collinearity from the figure, (3) prove it synthetically, (4) as a backup, prove it with complex/barycentric bash. - Solve G1–G4 from recent IMO Shortlists."},{"u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html#traps","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 05 · Geometry","h":"Traps","x":"- Proofs that only work for one configuration. Use directed angles. - Over-relying on bash. It's a time sink on hard problems, so keep it as plan B."},{"u":"math-olympiad/06-contest-practice.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","h":"06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","x":"Goal: turn knowledge into points under exam conditions. · Time: ongoing from week 3"},{"u":"math-olympiad/06-contest-practice.html#core-ideas","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Mock exams: 3 problems, 4.5 h, no notes, real timer. Use past national olympiads → IMO Shortlist (ISL) → past IMOs. - Grade strictly on the 0–7 IMO scale. A \"nearly complete\" proof usually earns 0–2, not 6. - Time allocation: first 30 min on reading all three problems and trying small cases. Spend the most time on the problem you're most likely to fully solve. - Partial credit: write down proven lemmas, reductions, and the answer with a construction. Graders reward substantial progress. - Post-mortem every mock: for each problem, what was the key idea, and what would have led you to it faste"},{"u":"math-olympiad/06-contest-practice.html#where-to-find-problems","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","h":"Where to find problems","x":"- ⭐ AoPS Contest Collections: almost every olympiad ever, with community solutions. - ⭐ IMO official site: problems & results and the IMO Shortlists (published after the following IMO). - Evan Chen's practice materials / OTIS excerpts and his MOHS hardness ratings (to pick problems at the right level). - Past papers of your own national olympiad. Ask the committee or past participants."},{"u":"math-olympiad/06-contest-practice.html#done-when","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · 06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] You consistently fully solve P1/P4-level problems in mocks and have partial progress on P2/P5."},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Math Olympiad Cheatsheet","x":"One page to recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN)."},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#strategies-01","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Strategies (→ 01)","x":"Small cases · draw it · work backwards · strengthen the induction · extremal element · invariant/monovariant · coloring/parity · pigeonhole · contradiction/descent · find the equality case / answer first"},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#algebra-02","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Algebra (→ 02)","x":"Tool Statement AM-GM \\frac{\\sum xi}{n}\\ge\\sqrt[n]{\\prod xi}, equality iff all are equal Cauchy–Schwarz (\\sum ai^2)(\\sum bi^2)\\ge(\\sum aibi)^2 Titu \\sum\\frac{ai^2}{bi}\\ge\\frac{(\\sum ai)^2}{\\sum bi} Schur (t0) \\sum a^t(a-b)(a-c)\\ge0 Jensen convex f: f(\\text{mean})\\le\\text{mean of }f Vieta (cubic) r+s+t=-\\frac ba, rs+st+tr=\\frac ca, rst=-\\frac da Integer poly a-b\\mid P(a)-P(b) FE plays x=y=0; y=0; y=x; y=-x; swap; injective/surjective; fixed points; verify the answer"},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#number-theory-03","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Number theory (→ 03)","x":"Tool Statement Bézout \\exists x,y: ax+by=\\gcd(a,b) Fermat / Euler a^{p-1}\\equiv1\\ (p) · a^{\\varphi(n)}\\equiv1\\ (n) for \\gcd(a,n)=1 Wilson (p-1)!\\equiv-1\\pmod p Order lemma a^k\\equiv1\\iff\\text{ord}(a)\\mid k; \\text{ord}p(a)\\mid p-1 gcd of powers \\gcd(a^m-1,a^n-1)=a^{\\gcd(m,n)}-1 LTE (odd p) p\\mid a-b, p\\nmid ab: vp(a^n-b^n)=vp(a-b)+vp(n) Legendre vp(n!)=\\sum\\lfloor n/p^k\\rfloor QR -1 is a QR mod odd p ⟺ p\\equiv1\\ (4); Euler's criterion \\left(\\frac ap\\right)\\equiv a^{(p-1)/2} Squares mod mod 4: {0,1} · mod 8: {0,1,4} · mod 3: {0,1} · mod 9 (cubes): {0,1,8}"},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#combinatorics-04","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Combinatorics (→ 04)","x":"- Stars & bars: \\binom{n+k-1}{k-1} · Derangements Dn=n!\\sum{i=0}^n\\frac{(-1)^i}{i!} · Catalan \\frac{1}{n+1}\\binom{2n}n - Handshake: \\sum\\deg=2 E · Tree: E = V -1 · Bipartite ⟺ no odd cycle · Hall: N(S) \\ge S \\ \\forall S - Max/min problems = bound + construction. Expected value < 1 ⇒ a good object exists."},{"u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#geometry-05","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Geometry (→ 05)","x":"- Inscribed angle = ½ arc · cyclic ⟺ opposite angles sum to 180° ⟺ equal angles on the same chord - Power of a point: PA\\cdot PB=PC\\cdot PD= PO^2-r^2 · radical axes of 3 circles concur - Euler line: O,G,H collinear, OG:GH=1:2 · nine-point circle center = midpoint of OH, radius R/2 - Reflections of H over the sides and side midpoints lie on the circumcircle · AH=2R\\cos A - Incenter–excenter: MA (arc midpoint of BC) is the center of the circle through B,C,I,IA - Ceva: \\frac{BD}{DC}\\frac{CE}{EA}\\frac{AF}{FB}=1 · Menelaus: same product =-1 (signed) · Ptolemy: AC\\cdot BD=AB\\cdot CD+AD\\cdot BC for c"},{"u":"math-olympiad/resources.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Resources","h":"Math Olympiad Resources","x":"⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free."},{"u":"math-olympiad/resources.html#books-by-stage","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Resources","h":"Books by stage","x":"Stage Book Use for Ramp ⭐ The Art and Craft of Problem Solving, Zeitz 💲 strategies, first proofs Ramp AoPS Volume 1 & 2, Intermediate Algebra, Intermediate Counting, Intro to NT 💲 filling gaps National ⭐ Problem-Solving Strategies, Engel 💲 huge problem bank by technique National ⭐ EGMO, Evan Chen 💲 geometry National Modern Olympiad Number Theory, Khurmi (free PDF, on AoPS) number theory National Problem Solving Methods in Combinatorics, Soberón 💲 combinatorics IMO Problems from the Book, Andreescu & Dospinescu 💲 hard algebra/NT IMO Lemmas in Olympiad Geometry, Andreescu et al. 💲 geometr"},{"u":"math-olympiad/resources.html#free-handouts-the-best-free-material","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Resources","h":"Free handouts (the best free material)","x":"- ⭐ Evan Chen's handouts: inequalities, orders, functional equations, geometry, proof writing. - ⭐ Yufei Zhao's olympiad handouts: from MOP (the US training camp). - Evan Chen's Napkin: higher math for olympiad students (optional, beyond the IMO)."},{"u":"math-olympiad/resources.html#problems-community","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Resources","h":"Problems & community","x":"- ⭐ AoPS Contest Collections: past olympiads with solutions. - ⭐ IMO official site: problems, rules, and results. - AoPS forums: ask questions, read solution discussions. - MOHS hardness scale (Evan Chen): difficulty ratings so you can pick problems at the right level."},{"u":"math-olympiad/resources.html#tools","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Resources","h":"Tools","x":"- GeoGebra: accurate geometry figures. - This repo's interactive tools: triangle centers, modular lab, flashcards."},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Math Olympiad: Progress & Log","x":""},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#key-dates-fill-these-in-first","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Key dates (fill these in first!)","x":"Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IMO 2027 July 2027 check eligibility"},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#status-board","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Status board","x":"⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done Module Status Problems solved Weakest sub-topic 01 Problem solving & proofs ⬜ 02 Algebra ⬜ 03 Number theory ⬜ 04 Combinatorics ⬜ 05 Geometry ⬜ 06 Contest practice ⬜ mocks:"},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#mock-exam-log","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Mock exam log","x":"Date Source P1 P2 P3 Total /21 Key lesson"},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#key-idea-notebook","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Key-idea notebook","x":"One line per problem. This is what you review before contests. Problem Area Key idea (1 line)"},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#session-log-newest-first","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Session log (newest first)","x":"<!--"},{"u":"math-olympiad/progress.html#yyyy-mm-dd-module-nn-x-h","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Progress log","h":"YYYY-MM-DD · module NN · X h","x":"- Solved: - Key ideas learned: - Stuck on: --"},{"u":"math-olympiad/interactive/triangle-centers.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Triangle Centers Explorer","x":"Module 05 · Geometry Drag a triangle and see O, I, G, H, the Euler line, and the nine-point circle. Check Euler's OI² = R(R−2r) live."},{"u":"math-olympiad/interactive/modular-arithmetic.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Modular Arithmetic Lab","x":"Module 03 · Number theory Power cycles on a clock, orders, primitive roots, Fermat, Euler, and Wilson."},{"u":"math-olympiad/interactive/flashcards.html","p":"🧮 Math Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Flashcards","x":"All modules · Recall ~45 theorems and techniques with spaced repetition."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/index.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Physics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IPhO","x":"Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and are strong at math and physics. Target: national team selection, then IPhO-level theory and experiment."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/index.html#read-first-eligibility-and-format","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format","x":"- IPhO contestants generally must be under 20 and not yet university students. As a senior, this cycle is likely your last. Check your national rules and selection dates now. - IPhO format: a theory exam (3 long multi-part problems, 5 h, 30 points) and an experimental exam (1–2 problems, 5 h, 20 points). 40% of the score is experimental, and many self-learners neglect it. - Problems are long and guided. Each problem builds a model step by step, so careful approximation and modeling beat obscure formulas."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/index.html#the-map","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"The map","x":"Module Time You can… 01 Math methods & estimation 1–2 wk approximations, dimensional analysis, ODEs, vector calculus basics 02 Mechanics 5–7 wk rigid bodies, oscillations, orbits, non-inertial frames, fluids 03 Thermodynamics & statistical physics 3–4 wk heat engines, kinetic theory, Boltzmann, radiation 04 Electromagnetism 5–7 wk fields, circuits, induction, image charges, EM waves 05 Waves & optics 2–3 wk interference, diffraction, geometric optics, Doppler 06 Modern physics 3–4 wk special relativity, quantum basics, atomic and nuclear physics 07 Experimental physics ongoing uncertainties, l"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/index.html#interactive-tools-interactive","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Interactive tools → interactive/","x":"- Orbit simulator: set the launch speed and see circles, ellipses, and escape. Kepler's laws and energy live. - Driven damped oscillator: resonance curve, phase lag, Q-factor, transients. - Wave interference: two-source pattern + double-slit/grating intensity. - Linear fit & uncertainty: least squares with slope/intercept errors, for the experimental exam. - Flashcards: formulas, constants, and standard results."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/index.html#weekly-routine-1012-h","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","x":"Block Do 3 × 2 h Theory: Kalda/Zhou handout section → 3–5 problems 1 × 2 h Experiment: a home experiment or a past IPhO/APhO experimental problem's data analysis 1 × 3 h Mock (from week 4): one full past theory problem in 1h40, then grade strictly with the official marking scheme 30 min Update progress.md and review flashcards Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 01 · Math Methods & Estimation","h":"01 · Math Methods & Estimation","x":"Goal: the mathematical moves olympiad physics uses constantly. · Time: 1–2 weeks (you're strong at math, so this is mostly about the physics habits)"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 01 · Math Methods & Estimation","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Use dimensional analysis to find the period of a pendulum and the speed of deep-water waves (v\\propto\\sqrt{g\\lambda}). - [ ] Expand (1+x)^n, \\sqrt{1+x}, \\sin x, \\cos x, e^x, \\ln(1+x) to second order. - [ ] Solve \\ddot x + 2\\gamma\\dot x + \\omega0^2 x = 0 in all three damping regimes. - [ ] Estimate the number of air molecules in your room, to within a factor of 3. - [ ] Compute the flux of a uniform field through a hemisphere using a symmetry argument, not an integral."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 01 · Math Methods & Estimation","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Dimensional analysis: write [Q] = M^aL^bT^c\\ldots and match exponents. It gives the answer up to a constant, and it's a quick check on every result. - Small-quantity expansions: (1+x)^n\\approx1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}2x^2. Keep the first non-vanishing order. Most IPhO tasks say \"to first order in \\epsilon\". - Fermi estimation: break it into factors and get each within ×3. - ODEs you must solve instantly: exponential growth/decay, SHM, damped/driven oscillator, \\dot v = -kv^2-type drag, separation of variables. - Vector calculus (light): gradient, divergence, curl as physical ideas. Gauss and Stokes"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 01 · Math Methods & Estimation","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Kevin Zhou's handouts: start with the introductory/preliminary handout and M1 (which covers the ODE toolkit). These are the best free olympiad physics materials. - Street-Fighting Mathematics, Sanjoy Mahajan: an open-access book (free PDF from MIT Press) on estimation and approximation. - Mathematical Methods for Physics and Engineering, Riley, Hobson & Bence 💲: reference only."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 01 · Math Methods & Estimation","h":"Build","x":"- Redo 10 textbook results using only dimensional analysis + one physical argument, and check them against the exact formula."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"02 · Mechanics","x":"Goal: the backbone of every IPhO (often 1–2 of the 3 theory problems). · Time: 5–7 weeks 👉 Interactive: Orbit simulator · Driven damped oscillator"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] A ladder leans on a frictionless wall with floor friction μ. Find the minimum angle before it slips. - [ ] A cylinder rolls without slipping down an incline. Find its acceleration two ways: forces/torques, and energy. - [ ] Find the period of small oscillations about a stable equilibrium of U(x) (\\omega^2 = U''(x0)/m). - [ ] Derive Kepler's third law for circular orbits, and the escape velocity. Explain the effective potential. - [ ] In a rotating frame, write down the centrifugal and Coriolis forces. - [ ] A rocket expels mass at relative speed u. Derive \\Delta v = u\\ln(m0/mf)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Kinematics: projectiles, relative motion, constraints (strings, rods, rolling: v = \\omega R). - Newton's laws: free-body diagrams. Choose axes wisely. Non-inertial frames add -m\\mathbf a{frame}, centrifugal m\\omega^2r, and Coriolis -2m\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\mathbf v. - Conservation laws: energy (work–energy, potential energy), momentum (collisions, CM frame), angular momentum (central forces, a skater pulling in her arms). Ask which quantity is conserved before writing equations. - Rigid bodies: moment of inertia (parallel/perpendicular axis theorems), torque \\boldsymbol\\tau = \\mathbf r\\tim"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Jaan Kalda: Mechanics study guide: a compact list of \"ideas\" with olympiad problems. Built specifically for IPhO. Also see Kevin Zhou's mechanics (M) handouts. - ⭐ Introduction to Classical Mechanics, David Morin 💲: the best problem-based textbook. Ch. 1–9. - 200 Puzzling Physics Problems, Gnädig, Honyek & Riley 💲 · Problems in General Physics, Irodov (classic and hard)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"Build","x":"- Solve every Kalda mechanics idea's associated problems. Then do 5 past IPhO mechanics problems under time pressure."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html#traps","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 02 · Mechanics","h":"Traps","x":"- Taking torques about a point that's accelerating (only the CM or a fixed point is safe for \\tau = I\\alpha). - Forgetting friction can point either way. Assume a direction and let the sign tell you."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","h":"03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","x":"Goal: gases, heat engines, entropy, and the Boltzmann factor. · Time: 3–4 weeks"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Derive the work done in isothermal and adiabatic expansions of an ideal gas. - [ ] Prove the Carnot efficiency 1 - Tc/Th and why no engine can beat it. - [ ] Derive pV = \\frac13 Nm\\langle v^2\\rangle from kinetic theory. State equipartition. - [ ] Use the Boltzmann factor to find the isothermal atmosphere's density profile. - [ ] Use Stefan–Boltzmann to estimate Earth's equilibrium temperature."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Ideal gas: pV = nRT. Internal energy U = \\frac f2 nRT (f = degrees of freedom). Cp - CV = R, \\gamma = Cp/CV. - First law: dU = \\delta Q - p\\,dV. Processes: isobaric, isochoric, isothermal, adiabatic (pV^\\gamma = const), polytropic. - Second law & entropy: dS = \\delta Q{rev}/T. The entropy of an isolated system never decreases. Carnot cycle, refrigerators, COP. - Kinetic theory: pressure from collisions, Maxwell speed distribution, mean free path \\lambda = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2 n\\sigma}, effusion. - Boltzmann factor: P \\propto e^{-E/kT}. Equipartition: \\frac12 kT per quadratic degree of freedom. - "},{"u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Kalda: Thermodynamics study guide + Kevin Zhou's thermodynamics (T) handouts. - An Introduction to Thermal Physics, Daniel Schroeder 💲: excellent and readable (ch. 1–4, 6). - Fundamentals of Physics, Halliday/Resnick/Walker 💲: the basics, if you need them."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","h":"Build","x":"- Draw p–V diagrams for 5 cycles and compute their efficiencies. Estimate Earth's temperature with and without albedo."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 04 · Electromagnetism","h":"04 · Electromagnetism","x":"Goal: fields, circuits, and induction at the level of IPhO's long problems. · Time: 5–7 weeks"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 04 · Electromagnetism","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Use Gauss's law for the field of a uniformly charged sphere (inside and outside) and an infinite plane. - [ ] Use an image charge: the force between a point charge and a grounded conducting plane. - [ ] Solve an RC charging circuit and an LC circuit. Give the energy stored in C and L. - [ ] Find \\mathbf B of a long straight wire and a solenoid with Ampère's law, and at the center of a loop with Biot–Savart. - [ ] A rod slides on rails in a uniform B. Find the EMF, current, and terminal velocity. - [ ] Find the motion of a charged particle in uniform E ⟂ B (drift velocity E/B)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 04 · Electromagnetism","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Electrostatics: Coulomb, superposition, \\mathbf E = -\\nabla V. Gauss's law \\oint\\mathbf E\\cdot d\\mathbf A = Q/\\varepsilon0 (use symmetry!). - Conductors: E = 0 inside, the surface is an equipotential. Method of images. Capacitance, energy \\frac12CV^2, energy density \\frac12\\varepsilon0E^2. - Dielectrics: \\varepsilonr, polarization (qualitative/basic). - Circuits: Kirchhoff's laws, series/parallel, Thevenin equivalent, infinite ladders (self-similarity), RC/RL/LC/RLC transients, AC with complex impedance (ZL = i\\omega L, ZC = 1/(i\\omega C)), non-linear elements (diodes, graphically). - Magnet"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 04 · Electromagnetism","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Kalda: Electricity & Magnetism study guide + Kevin Zhou's E&M (E) handouts. - ⭐ Electricity and Magnetism, Purcell & Morin (3rd ed.) 💲: the olympiad-standard E&M textbook. - Introduction to Electrodynamics, Griffiths 💲: ch. 1–7, for more mathematical depth."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 04 · Electromagnetism","h":"Build","x":"- Solve 5 infinite/symmetric circuit puzzles. Derive the field of a uniformly magnetized sphere (stretch). Do 3 past IPhO E&M problems."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 05 · Waves & Optics","h":"05 · Waves & Optics","x":"Goal: interference, diffraction, and geometric optics. These show up in both the theory and the experiment. · Time: 2–3 weeks 👉 Interactive: Wave interference & diffraction"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 05 · Waves & Optics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Derive the double-slit fringe spacing \\Delta y = \\lambda L/d. - [ ] Find the condition for thin-film constructive reflection (including the phase flip). - [ ] Locate the image for a thin lens and a spherical mirror. Trace the rays for a two-lens system. - [ ] Use the Doppler formula for a moving source and a moving observer (sound). - [ ] Find the resolving power of a telescope (Rayleigh criterion 1.22\\lambda/D)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 05 · Waves & Optics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Waves: y = A\\sin(kx - \\omega t), v = \\omega/k = f\\lambda. The wave equation. String speed v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu}, sound speed. Standing waves and harmonics. Beats. - Superposition: phase difference \\Delta\\phi = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}\\Delta(\\text{path}) + reflection phase. Constructive at \\Delta\\phi = 2\\pi m. - Interference: double slit, thin films (π phase shift on reflection off a higher-n medium), Newton's rings, Michelson interferometer. - Diffraction: single slit minima at a\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda, diffraction grating maxima at d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda. Intensity = (single-slit envelope) × (multi-sl"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 05 · Waves & Optics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Kalda: Optics/waves material + Kevin Zhou's waves/optics handouts. - Optics, Eugene Hecht 💲 for reference · Falstad wave simulations to play with ripples."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 05 · Waves & Optics","h":"Build","x":"- Measure the track spacing of a CD/DVD with a laser pointer (diffraction grating). A classic home experiment for module 07, too."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 06 · Modern Physics","h":"06 · Modern Physics","x":"Goal: special relativity, quantum basics, and atomic/nuclear physics at IPhO level. · Time: 3–4 weeks"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 06 · Modern Physics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Derive time dilation and length contraction from the Lorentz transformation. - [ ] Use 4-momentum invariance to find the threshold energy of a particle-creation reaction. - [ ] Compute the photoelectric stopping potential, and the Compton shift. - [ ] Use the Bohr model to derive the hydrogen energy levels En = -13.6\\,\\text{eV}/n^2. - [ ] Use Heisenberg to estimate the ground-state energy of a particle in a box. - [ ] Compute the activity after t given the half-life. Compute a nuclear reaction's Q-value."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 06 · Modern Physics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Special relativity: postulates; Lorentz transformation, \\gamma = 1/\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}; time dilation, length contraction, relativity of simultaneity; velocity addition u' = \\frac{u-v}{1-uv/c^2}; E = \\gamma mc^2, \\mathbf p = \\gamma m\\mathbf v; invariant E^2 - (pc)^2 = (mc^2)^2. Use invariants for collisions and decays. Relativistic Doppler. - Quantum basics: photon E = hf, p = h/\\lambda; photoelectric effect; Compton \\Delta\\lambda = \\frac{h}{mec}(1-\\cos\\theta); de Broglie \\lambda = h/p; uncertainty \\Delta x\\Delta p \\gtrsim \\hbar/2 (for estimates); particle in a box En = \\frac{n^2h^2}{8mL^2}; tu"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 06 · Modern Physics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Kevin Zhou's relativity (R) and modern-physics handouts + Kalda. - Spacetime Physics, Taylor & Wheeler 💲 (relativity done beautifully) · Morin's Classical Mechanics ch. 11–14 (relativity). - Feynman Lectures, Vol. III for quantum intuition (free online)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html#build","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 06 · Modern Physics","h":"Build","x":"- 10 relativity problems solved with invariants only (no frame juggling). Compare them against the brute-force method."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 07 · Experimental Physics","h":"07 · Experimental Physics","x":"Goal: earn the 20 experimental points. It's the most trainable part of the IPhO. · Time: ~2 h/week, ongoing 👉 Interactive: Linear fit & uncertainty tool"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html#skip-test","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 07 · Experimental Physics","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Propagate uncertainty for g = 4\\pi^2L/T^2 given \\Delta L and \\Delta T. - [ ] Linearize T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}, I = I0e^{-\\mu x}, and y = ax^n so a straight-line graph gives the unknowns. - [ ] Find a slope and its uncertainty from a least-squares fit (or the max/min slope method). - [ ] Explain systematic vs random error, and how to reduce each. - [ ] Explain why you should measure 20 oscillations instead of 1."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html#core-ideas","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 07 · Experimental Physics","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Uncertainty: report x = \\bar x \\pm \\Delta x with 1–2 significant figures in \\Delta x, and the value rounded to match. - Sum/difference: absolute errors add in quadrature: \\Delta(a\\pm b) = \\sqrt{\\Delta a^2+\\Delta b^2}. - Product/power: relative errors add in quadrature: \\frac{\\Delta f}{f} = \\sqrt{\\left(n\\frac{\\Delta a}{a}\\right)^2 + \\left(m\\frac{\\Delta b}{b}\\right)^2} for f = a^nb^m. - Repeated measurements: standard error of the mean \\sigma/\\sqrt N. - Linearization: transform the data so the theory predicts a straight line (T^2 vs L; \\ln I vs x; \\ln y vs \\ln x). The slope/intercept then give"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html#learn-from","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 07 · Experimental Physics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Past IPhO / APhO / EuPhO experimental problems with marking schemes (see resources). Practice the data-analysis parts even without the apparatus. - ⭐ Kalda: Experimental study guide. - An Introduction to Error Analysis, John Taylor 💲: the standard text (ch. 1–8)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html#home-experiments-cheap","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · 07 · Experimental Physics","h":"Home experiments (cheap)","x":"1. Pendulum → g to < 1% (linearize T^2 vs L). 2. CD/DVD as a diffraction grating with a laser pointer → track spacing. 3. Cooling coffee cup → Newton's law of cooling (fit \\ln(T - T{room}) vs t). 4. A bouncing ball → coefficient of restitution from the sound of the bounces (phone audio recorder). 5. A smartphone sensor app (e.g. phyphox) → acceleration, rotation, magnetic field, sound frequency."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Physics Olympiad Cheatsheet","x":"One-page recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#constants-to-3-sig-figs","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Constants (to 3 sig figs)","x":"c = 3.00\\times10^8 m/s · G = 6.67\\times10^{-11} · h = 6.63\\times10^{-34} J·s · \\hbar = 1.05\\times10^{-34} · kB = 1.38\\times10^{-23} J/K · NA = 6.02\\times10^{23} · R = 8.31 J/(mol·K) · e = 1.60\\times10^{-19} C · me = 9.11\\times10^{-31} kg · mp = 1.67\\times10^{-27} kg · \\varepsilon0 = 8.85\\times10^{-12} · \\mu0 = 4\\pi\\times10^{-7} · \\sigma = 5.67\\times10^{-8} W/(m²K⁴) · 1 eV = 1.60\\times10^{-19} J"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#approximations-01","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Approximations (→ 01)","x":"(1+x)^n\\approx1+nx · \\sin x\\approx x - x^3/6 · \\cos x\\approx1-x^2/2 · \\ln(1+x)\\approx x - x^2/2 · e^x\\approx1+x+x^2/2 Small oscillations about x0: \\omega^2 = U''(x0)/m"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#mechanics-02","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Mechanics (→ 02)","x":"- Moments of inertia: rod (center) \\frac1{12}ML^2 · rod (end) \\frac13ML^2 · disk \\frac12MR^2 · ring MR^2 · solid sphere \\frac25MR^2 · shell \\frac23MR^2 · parallel axis I = I{cm}+Md^2 - Rolling down an incline: a = \\frac{g\\sin\\theta}{1+I/(mR^2)} - Rotating frame: \\mathbf F{cf} = m\\omega^2\\mathbf r\\perp, \\mathbf F{cor} = -2m\\boldsymbol\\omega\\times\\mathbf v - Orbits: v{circ} = \\sqrt{GM/r} · v{esc} = \\sqrt{2GM/r} · T^2 = \\frac{4\\pi^2}{GM}a^3 · E = -\\frac{GMm}{2a} · U{eff} = -\\frac{GMm}r+\\frac{L^2}{2mr^2} - Rocket: \\Delta v = u\\ln\\frac{m0}{mf} · Damped: \\omegad = \\sqrt{\\omega0^2-\\gamma^2} (for \\ddo"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#thermo-03","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Thermo (→ 03)","x":"pV = nRT · U = \\frac f2nRT · Cp-CV = R · adiabatic pV^\\gamma = const, TV^{\\gamma-1} = const W{iso} = nRT\\ln\\frac{V2}{V1} · \\eta{Carnot} = 1-\\frac{Tc}{Th} · dS = \\delta Q/T · P\\propto e^{-E/kT} · v{rms} = \\sqrt{3kT/m} · P = \\sigma AT^4 · \\lambda{max}T = 2.90\\times10^{-3} m·K"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#em-04","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"E&M (→ 04)","x":"- Sphere: E = \\frac{kQ}{r^2} outside, \\frac{kQr}{R^3} inside (uniform) · plane \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon0} · capacitor C = \\frac{\\varepsilon0A}d, U = \\frac12CV^2 - Wire B = \\frac{\\mu0I}{2\\pi r} · loop center \\frac{\\mu0I}{2R} · solenoid \\mu0nI · UL = \\frac12LI^2 · u = \\frac{\\varepsilon0E^2}2+\\frac{B^2}{2\\mu0} - \\tau{RC} = RC · \\tau{RL} = L/R · \\omega{LC} = 1/\\sqrt{LC} · ZL = i\\omega L, ZC = \\frac{1}{i\\omega C} - Cyclotron r = \\frac{mv}{qB}, \\omega = \\frac{qB}m · E⟂B drift v = E/B · \\mathcal E = -\\frac{d\\Phi}{dt}, motional \\mathcal E = vBL"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#waves-optics-05","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Waves & optics (→ 05)","x":"Double slit \\Delta y = \\frac{\\lambda L}d · grating d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda · single-slit minima a\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda · Rayleigh 1.22\\lambda/D · thin lens \\frac1f = \\frac1{do}+\\frac1{di} · Snell n1\\sin\\theta1 = n2\\sin\\theta2 · Brewster \\tan\\thetaB = n2/n1 · string v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu} · Doppler f' = f\\frac{v\\pm vo}{v\\mp vs}"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#modern-06","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Modern (→ 06)","x":"\\gamma = (1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2} · E^2 = (pc)^2+(mc^2)^2 · u' = \\frac{u-v}{1-uv/c^2} · relativistic Doppler f' = f\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\beta}{1-\\beta}} (approaching) E = hf · \\lambda = h/p · Compton \\Delta\\lambda = \\frac{h}{mec}(1-\\cos\\theta) · Bohr En = -\\frac{13.6\\text{ eV}}{n^2} · box En = \\frac{n^2h^2}{8mL^2} · hc = 1240 eV·nm · N = N02^{-t/T{1/2}}"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#experiment-07","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Experiment (→ 07)","x":"f = a^nb^m: \\frac{\\Delta f}{f} = \\sqrt{(n\\frac{\\Delta a}a)^2+(m\\frac{\\Delta b}b)^2} · mean error \\sigma/\\sqrt N · linearize: T^2 vs L, \\ln y vs x (exponential), \\ln y vs \\ln x (power law)"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Physics Olympiad Resources","x":"⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html#the-core-stack","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Resources","h":"The core stack","x":"1. ⭐ Kevin Zhou's olympiad handouts: complete, free, problem-rich (mechanics, E&M, thermo, waves, relativity, modern). The best single resource. 2. ⭐ Jaan Kalda's study guides: compact \"idea lists\" (kinematics, mechanics, E&M, thermodynamics, experimental) with IPhO-level problems. 3. ⭐ Past IPhO problems with official solutions and marking schemes (below)."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html#textbooks-pick-per-module","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Textbooks (💲, pick per module)","x":"Book Module Introduction to Classical Mechanics, Morin 02 (and relativity, 06) Electricity and Magnetism, Purcell & Morin 04 An Introduction to Thermal Physics, Schroeder 03 An Introduction to Error Analysis, Taylor 07 200 Puzzling Physics Problems / 200 More Puzzling…, Gnädig et al. all (problem books) Physics Olympiad: Basic to Advanced Exercises (Japan PhO committee) all Problems in General Physics, Irodov all (hard drill) Fundamentals of Physics, Halliday/Resnick/Walker gap filling"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html#past-problems","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Past problems","x":"- ⭐ IPhO official site and the IPhO unofficial archive: past theory and experimental problems, with solutions. - APhO (Asian), EuPhO (European), and USAPhO (AAPT Physics Team past exams): excellent intermediate-level sets. - Physics Cup (Kalda): very hard, creative problems, with an online competition each year. - Your country's national olympiad archive."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html#free-extras","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Free extras","x":"- The Feynman Lectures on Physics: deep intuition. - Falstad simulations: circuits, waves, E&M. - phyphox: turns your phone into a lab (for module 07). - This repo's interactive tools."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Physics Olympiad: Progress & Log","x":""},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#key-dates","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Key dates","x":"Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IPhO 2027 July 2027 check eligibility"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#status-board","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Status board","x":"⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done Module Status Kalda ideas done Past IPhO problems done 01 Math methods ⬜ 02 Mechanics ⬜ 03 Thermo & stat ⬜ 04 E&M ⬜ 05 Waves & optics ⬜ 06 Modern ⬜ 07 Experimental ⬜ home experiments:"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#mock-log","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Mock log","x":"Date Problem (year/no.) Type Score / max Time Lesson theory / exp"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#ideas-notebook","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Ideas notebook","x":"Problem Key idea / trick"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#session-log-newest-first","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Session log (newest first)","x":"<!--"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html#yyyy-mm-dd-module-nn-x-h","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"YYYY-MM-DD · module NN · X h","x":"- Did: - Key idea: - Fuzzy: --"},{"u":"physics-olympiad/interactive/orbits.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Orbit Simulator","x":"Module 02 · Mechanics Circles, ellipses, escape. Kepler II and III verified live, with energy and angular momentum readouts."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/interactive/oscillator.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Driven Damped Oscillator","x":"Module 02 · Oscillations Transients, resonance curve, phase lag, Q-factor."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/interactive/interference.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Wave Interference","x":"Module 05 · Waves & optics Two-source ripple tank, plus N-slit grating intensity with missing orders."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/interactive/linear-fit.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Linear Fit & Uncertainty","x":"Module 07 · Experiment Linearize, fit, and get slope ± error. Pendulum, decay, and power-law presets."},{"u":"physics-olympiad/interactive/flashcards.html","p":"⚛️ Physics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Flashcards","x":"All modules · Recall ~45 standard results with spaced repetition."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Informatics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IOI","x":"Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and know C++ and the STL. Target: national team selection, then IOI-level algorithms."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html#read-first-eligibility-and-format","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format","x":"- IOI contestants must generally be secondary-school students (or have finished very recently) and under 20. As a senior, this is likely your last cycle. Check your national selection dates now. - IOI format: 2 contest days × 3 problems × 5 hours, 100 points per problem, split into subtasks. Submissions are judged live with full feedback. C++ is the language to use. - Partial scoring changes strategy: collecting subtasks across all problems usually beats sinking 5 h into one full solution."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html#the-map","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"The map","x":"Module Time You can… 01 C++ for competitive programming 1 wk fast I/O, STL fluency, templates, debugging, stress testing 02 Complexity & core techniques 3–4 wk choose an algorithm from the constraints; binary search, two pointers, prefix sums, greedy 03 Data structures 4–5 wk DSU, Fenwick tree, segment tree (lazy), sparse table, monotonic stack 04 Graphs & trees 5–6 wk shortest paths, MST, SCC, LCA, tree DP 05 Dynamic programming 5–7 wk from knapsack to bitmask, tree DP, and DP optimizations 06 Math, strings & geometry 3–4 wk modular arithmetic, combinatorics, hashing, KMP/Z, convex hull 07 IO"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html#interactive-tools-interactive","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Interactive tools → interactive/","x":"- Graph search visualizer: draw walls/weights on a grid. Step through BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, and A. - Segment tree visualizer: watch the build, point update, and range query visit exactly O(log n) nodes. - DP table animator: LCS, edit distance, 0/1 knapsack filled cell by cell, with traceback. - Flashcards: complexities, patterns, and \"constraints → algorithm\" recall."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html#weekly-routine-1012-h","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Overview & roadmap","h":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","x":"Block Do 3 × 2 h Topic: read the USACO Guide section → solve 4–6 problems (CSES + guide problems) 1 × 3 h Virtual contest: Codeforces Div 2 / AtCoder ABC/ARC, or a past olympiad (oj.uz), under real time 1 × 1.5 h Upsolve: solve the contest problems you missed, after reading the editorial 15 min Log in progress.md: rating, solved count, one idea per problem Rough benchmarks (vary by country): strong national level ≈ Codeforces 1800–2100; IOI medal range ≈ 2100–2600+. The rating is a proxy, so spend your time on olympiad-style problems with subtasks. Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md ·"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","h":"01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","x":"Goal: turn your STL knowledge into contest speed. · Time: ~1 week"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Write a template with fast I/O, long long aliases, and a multi-test-case loop. - [ ] Sort a vector by second element descending with a lambda. - [ ] Use lowerbound on a sorted vector and on a set, and explain the complexity difference vs std::lowerbound(set.begin(), …). - [ ] Explain when int overflows (≈ 2.1 × 10⁹) and when to use long long. - [ ] Write a stress test: brute force vs fast solution on random inputs, stopping at the first mismatch."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Template: cpp include using namespace std; using ll = long long; int main() { ios::syncwithstdio(false); cin.tie(nullptr); int t = 1; // cin t; while (t--) { / solve / } } - STL you'll use constantly: vector, array, pair/tuple, sort with a custom comparator, lowerbound/upperbound, set/multiset/map (O(log n)), unorderedmap (beware anti-hash tests), priorityqueue (max-heap by default; use greater< for a min-heap), deque, bitset, builtinpopcount, iota, accumulate, nextpermutation. - Overflow: products of two ints ≤ 10⁹ need ll. Use 1LL a b. - Debugging: compile with -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=add"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ USACO Guide: General & Bronze: setup, C++ basics for CP, debugging, and stress testing. - Competitive Programmer's Handbook (Laaksonen), ch. 1–4. - cppreference for exact STL semantics."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","h":"Build","x":"- Your personal template + a stress.sh script (generator, brute, solution, compare). Keep both in a repo."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","h":"02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","x":"Goal: read the constraints, infer the target complexity, and use the standard techniques. · Time: 3–4 weeks"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Given n ≤ 2·10⁵, name an acceptable complexity and an unacceptable one. - [ ] Binary search on the answer: minimum maximum load when splitting an array into k parts. - [ ] Two pointers: the longest subarray with sum ≤ S (positive numbers). - [ ] Prefix sums + difference arrays: apply q range-add updates, then print the array, in O(n + q). - [ ] Prove a greedy algorithm correct with an exchange argument (e.g. activity selection). - [ ] Generate all subsets of n ≤ 20 elements with bitmasks."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","h":"Core ideas","x":"- The constraints → complexity table (≈ 10⁸ simple ops/second): n target ≤ 10–12 O(n!) ≤ 20–25 O(2ⁿ · n) ≤ 500 O(n³) ≤ 5000 O(n²) ≤ 10⁶ O(n log n) ≤ 10⁸ or larger O(n), O(log n), O(1) math - Sorting + custom comparators. Coordinate compression. - Binary search: on sorted arrays, and on the answer (a monotone predicate \"is x feasible?\"). Write it the same way every time (lo always bad, hi always good). - Two pointers / sliding window: when moving the right end only ever moves the left end forward. - Prefix sums (1D/2D), difference arrays, prefix XOR/min. - Greedy: sort by the right key and prov"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ USACO Guide: Bronze → Silver: complete search, sorting, binary search, two pointers, prefix sums, greedy. - ⭐ CSES Problem Set: \"Introductory Problems\" and \"Sorting and Searching\" (do all ~45). - Competitive Programmer's Handbook, ch. 5–8."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","h":"Build","x":"- Finish CSES Introductory + Sorting and Searching. Track the time per problem."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 03 · Data Structures","h":"03 · Data Structures","x":"Goal: answer queries fast. These structures appear in a large share of IOI tasks. · Time: 4–5 weeks 👉 Interactive: Segment tree visualizer"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 03 · Data Structures","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Implement DSU with path compression + union by size. Explain its α(n) complexity. - [ ] Implement a Fenwick tree (BIT) for point update / prefix sum. Explain i & -i. - [ ] Implement a segment tree with lazy propagation for range add + range sum. - [ ] Answer static range-minimum queries in O(1) with a sparse table. - [ ] Use a monotonic stack to find the next greater element for every index in O(n). - [ ] Use a sliding-window maximum with a deque."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 03 · Data Structures","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Stacks / queues / deques: monotonic stack (next greater/smaller, largest rectangle in a histogram), monotonic deque (sliding window min/max). - Heaps (priorityqueue): k-th largest, merging sorted lists, event simulation. - Ordered sets/maps: set, multiset, and map for dynamic order statistics by value. The policy-based orderedset (GNU pbds) gives the k-th element. - DSU (union-find): connectivity, Kruskal, offline queries. Near-O(1) per operation. - Fenwick tree: prefix sums with point updates, O(log n), very short code. Variants: range update/point query, 2D. - Segment tree: any associative"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 03 · Data Structures","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ USACO Guide: Silver → Gold: DSU, prefix sums, Fenwick/segment trees. - ⭐ Codeforces EDU: Segment Tree (parts 1–2): lectures + graded practice. - cp-algorithms: Data structures: segment tree, Fenwick, DSU, sparse table. - CSES: \"Range Queries\" section."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 03 · Data Structures","h":"Build","x":"- Write each structure from memory 3 times on different days. Solve all of CSES Range Queries."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 04 · Graphs & Trees","h":"04 · Graphs & Trees","x":"Goal: model problems as graphs and pick the right traversal or shortest-path algorithm. · Time: 5–6 weeks 👉 Interactive: Graph search visualizer"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 04 · Graphs & Trees","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] BFS on a grid with walls: the shortest path length and the path itself. - [ ] Explain why Dijkstra fails with negative edges, and what to use instead. - [ ] Topological sort with Kahn's algorithm. Detect a cycle. - [ ] Find strongly connected components (Tarjan or Kosaraju). - [ ] LCA with binary lifting in O(log n) per query. - [ ] Find the diameter of a tree with two BFS/DFS passes."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 04 · Graphs & Trees","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Representation: adjacency lists vector (or pair for weights). Grids are implicit graphs. - DFS: connected components, cycle detection, entry/exit times, bridges and articulation points (low-link). - BFS: unweighted shortest paths, multi-source BFS, 0-1 BFS with a deque. - Shortest paths: Dijkstra O((n+m) log n) for non-negative weights · Bellman–Ford O(nm) with negative edges/cycles · Floyd–Warshall O(n³) all pairs. - DAGs: topological sort, DP over a DAG (longest path, counting paths). - MST: Kruskal (sort + DSU) or Prim. - SCC: Tarjan/Kosaraju → the condensation DAG. 2-SAT comes from SCC. "},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 04 · Graphs & Trees","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ USACO Guide: Silver/Gold graphs & trees. - ⭐ CSES: \"Graph Algorithms\" and \"Tree Algorithms\" sections. - cp-algorithms: Graphs · Competitive Programmer's Handbook, ch. 11–20."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 04 · Graphs & Trees","h":"Build","x":"- Solve the CSES graph section (≈ 36 problems) and tree section (≈ 16). Implement Dijkstra, LCA, and SCC from memory."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 05 · Dynamic Programming","h":"05 · Dynamic Programming","x":"Goal: IOI's favorite topic. Define states well, then optimize. · Time: 5–7 weeks 👉 Interactive: DP table animator"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 05 · Dynamic Programming","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] 0/1 knapsack in O(nW) time and O(W) memory (loop direction!). - [ ] LIS in O(n log n). - [ ] Edit distance / LCS with path reconstruction. - [ ] Interval DP: the minimum cost to merge stones / matrix-chain multiplication. - [ ] Bitmask DP: TSP on n ≤ 20 in O(2ⁿn²). - [ ] Tree DP: a maximum independent set on a tree."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 05 · Dynamic Programming","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Recipe: (1) state: what do I need to know about the past? (2) transition (3) base cases (4) order of computation (5) answer location. Write the state in words first. - Classic families: - Linear: Fibonacci-like, max subarray, coin change (count vs. minimize), LIS. - Knapsack: 0/1 (iterate weight down), unbounded (iterate up), bounded (binary splitting). - Grid / two-sequence: paths, LCS, edit distance. - Interval: dp[l][r], with the split point inside. - Bitmask: dp[mask][last], subsets, SOS DP. - Tree DP: dp[v][state] from children. Rerooting for \"for every root\". - Digit DP: count numbers "},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 05 · Dynamic Programming","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ AtCoder Educational DP Contest: 26 classic problems A–Z. Do all of them. - ⭐ USACO Guide: Gold DP sections + CSES \"Dynamic Programming\". - cp-algorithms: DP (CHT, D&C DP, Knuth) · Errichto's DP videos on YouTube."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 05 · Dynamic Programming","h":"Build","x":"- AtCoder DP contest A–Z + CSES DP section. For each, write the state in one sentence in your progress log."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","h":"06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","x":"Goal: the math and string toolkit (your math-olympiad training helps here). · Time: 3–4 weeks"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html#skip-test","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Fast modular exponentiation and the modular inverse via Fermat (p prime). - [ ] Precompute factorials and inverse factorials to answer nCr mod p in O(1). - [ ] Sieve of Eratosthenes up to 10⁷. A smallest-prime-factor sieve for fast factorization. - [ ] Polynomial rolling hash: compare substrings in O(1). Explain collision risk and double hashing. - [ ] KMP prefix function or Z-function: find all occurrences of a pattern. - [ ] Convex hull (monotone chain) using the cross product."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html#core-ideas","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Number theory: gcd/lcm, extended Euclid, modular arithmetic mod 10⁹+7, modpow, inverses, sieve, factorization, Euler φ, CRT. - Combinatorics: nCr mod p, stars and bars, inclusion–exclusion, Catalan numbers, counting with DP. - Matrix exponentiation: linear recurrences in O(k³ log n). - Probability/expectation: linearity of expectation, expected-value DP. - Strings: hashing, KMP / prefix function, Z-function, trie (also the binary trie for max XOR), Manacher (palindromes), suffix array (advanced), Aho–Corasick (advanced). - Geometry (light at IOI): use integer cross products for orientation, "},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ cp-algorithms: algebra, combinatorics, strings, geometry sections. - ⭐ CSES: \"Mathematics\" and \"String Algorithms\" sections. - USACO Guide: Gold/Platinum (math, strings)."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","h":"Build","x":"- Add modint, combinatorics, sieve, hashing, KMP, and convex hull snippets to your personal library (kactl-style). Test each one."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","h":"07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","x":"Goal: convert knowledge into IOI points, and learn the advanced techniques that separate medal colors. · Time: ongoing"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html#contest-strategy-the-highest-leverage-skill","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","h":"Contest strategy (the highest-leverage skill)","x":"- First 20–30 min: read all three problems. Note every subtask and its points. - Bank points: implement the easy subtasks of every problem before going deep. 3 × 40 points beats 1 × 100. - Subtasks are hints: \"n ≤ 1000\", \"the tree is a line\", \"all values distinct\" each show a step toward the full solution. - Think before coding. Prove (or at least stress-test) your idea. With live feedback at IOI, use it to verify. - Keep the brute force for stress testing. Don't delete old working submissions. - Problem types to practice: batch (function implementation), interactive (minimize the queries), co"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html#advanced-techniques-learn-after-0106","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","h":"Advanced techniques (learn after 01–06)","x":"- Trees: heavy-light decomposition (HLD), centroid decomposition, small-to-large merging (DSU on tree), virtual trees. - Data structures: persistent segment tree, segment tree beats (rare), Li Chao tree, merge-sort tree, parallel binary search, offline divide & conquer (CDQ). - Sqrt techniques: sqrt decomposition on queries, Mo's algorithm (with updates), heavy/light threshold splitting. - Graphs: max flow / min cut / bipartite matching (rarer at IOI but useful), bridges/2-edge-connected components, Euler tours. - DP: CHT, D&C optimization, the Aliens trick, DP over subsets / profiles."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html#learn-from","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Past IOI problems on oj.uz (judged with subtasks) plus past national/regional olympiads (APIO, BOI, CEOI, JOI). The best practice material. - ⭐ USACO Guide: Platinum & Advanced. - IOI official site: the syllabus, past tasks, and solutions. - Codeforces (Div 1/2) and AtCoder (ARC/AGC) for contest practice."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html#build","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · 07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","h":"Build","x":"- Simulate full IOI days: 3 past-IOI problems, 5 h, scored by subtasks on oj.uz. Log the scores."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Informatics Olympiad Cheatsheet","x":"One-page recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN)."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#constraints-complexity-108-opss-02","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Constraints → complexity (≈10⁸ ops/s) (→ 02)","x":"n ≤ 12: n! · n ≤ 25: 2ⁿ·n · n ≤ 500: n³ · n ≤ 5000: n² · n ≤ 10⁶: n log n · bigger: n, log n, O(1)"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#snippets-0106","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Snippets (→ 01–06)","x":"// binary search on answer: smallest x with ok(x) true (ok monotone false→true) ll lo = L - 1, hi = R; // ok(lo) false, ok(hi) true while (hi - lo 1) { ll m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; (ok(m) ? hi : lo) = m; } // DSU int p[N], sz[N]; int find(int x){ return p[x]==x ? x : p[x]=find(p[x]); } bool unite(int a,int b){ a=find(a); b=find(b); if(a==b) return false; if(sz[a]<sz[b]) swap(a,b); p[b]=a; sz[a]+=sz[b]; return true; } // Fenwick (1-indexed) void add(int i, ll v){ for(; i<=n; i+=i&-i) bit[i]+=v; } ll sum(int i){ ll s=0; for(; i0; i-=i&-i) s+=bit[i]; return s; } // modpow ll pw(ll b, ll e, ll m){ l"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#complexities-0304","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Complexities (→ 03–04)","x":"Structure / algorithm Time DSU (path compr. + size) α(n) ≈ O(1) Fenwick / segment tree op O(log n) Sparse table build / query O(n log n) / O(1) BFS / DFS O(n + m) Dijkstra (binary heap) O((n + m) log n) Bellman–Ford / Floyd–Warshall O(nm) / O(n³) Kruskal O(m log m) Tarjan SCC / topo sort O(n + m) LCA binary lifting O(n log n) build, O(log n) query KMP / Z-function O(n + m) Sieve O(n log log n)"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#dp-patterns-05","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"DP patterns (→ 05)","x":"- 0/1 knapsack: for item: for w = W..wt: dp[w] = max(dp[w], dp[w-wt]+val) (down!) · unbounded: up - LIS: auto it = lowerbound(d.begin(), d.end(), x); if(it==d.end()) d.pushback(x); else it = x; - Bitmask TSP: dp[mask][v] · interval: dp[l][r] = min over k · tree: dp[v][0/1] from children"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html#pitfalls","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Cheatsheet","h":"Pitfalls","x":"int overflow (use 1LL) · uninitialized arrays across tests · recursion depth (~10⁵–10⁶) · endl flushes (use '\\n') · comparing doubles · off-by-one in binary search · unorderedmap hacks"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Informatics Olympiad Resources","x":"⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html#the-core-stack","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"The core stack","x":"1. ⭐ USACO Guide: a structured curriculum from Bronze to Platinum, with problems per topic. Use it as the spine of modules 01–07. 2. ⭐ CSES Problem Set (300+ classic problems) + Competitive Programmer's Handbook (free PDF, Laaksonen). 3. ⭐ AtCoder Educational DP Contest: for module 05. 4. ⭐ Past IOI and olympiad tasks on oj.uz: subtask-judged practice."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html#references","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"References","x":"- cp-algorithms: an encyclopedia of algorithms with proofs and code. - KACTL: a well-tested code library (great for learning clean implementations). - Guide to Competitive Programming, Laaksonen 💲 (the expanded handbook) · Algorithms, Erickson (free) for proofs. - cppreference."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html#practice-contests","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Practice & contests","x":"- Codeforces: weekly contests, plus the EDU section (segment tree, DSU, binary search courses). - AtCoder: ABC (beginner–intermediate), ARC/AGC (hard, math-flavored). - IOI official site: the syllabus and past tasks. - Past APIO, BOI, CEOI, JOI, EGOI, and your national olympiad problems (most are on oj.uz)."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html#video","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"Video","x":"- Errichto (YouTube): problem solving, DP, binary search. - Colin Galen, William Lin (YouTube): contest walkthroughs and how they practice."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html#this-repo","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Resources","h":"This repo","x":"- Interactive tools: graph search, segment tree, DP table, flashcards."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Informatics Olympiad: Progress & Log","x":""},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#key-dates","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Key dates","x":"Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IOI 2027 summer 2027 check eligibility"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#status-board","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Status board","x":"⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done Module Status CSES solved Other problems 01 C++ for CP ⬜ — template + stress script: ⬜ 02 Core techniques ⬜ / 03 Data structures ⬜ / 04 Graphs & trees ⬜ / 05 DP ⬜ / AtCoder DP: /26 06 Math, strings, geometry ⬜ / 07 IOI skills & advanced ⬜ — IOI days simulated:"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#rating-log","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Rating log","x":"Date Platform Contest Solved Rating after"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#ioi-day-simulations","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"IOI-day simulations","x":"Date Problems (year) P1 P2 P3 Total /300 Lesson"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#idea-log-one-line-per-problem","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Idea log (one line per problem)","x":"Problem Topic Key idea"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#session-log-newest-first","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"Session log (newest first)","x":"<!--"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html#yyyy-mm-dd-module-nn-x-h","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Progress log","h":"YYYY-MM-DD · module NN · X h","x":"- Solved: - Key idea: - Bug I made (so I don't repeat it): --"},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/interactive/graph-search.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Graph Search Visualizer","x":"Module 04 · Graphs Draw walls and weights. Compare BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, and A* step by step."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/interactive/segment-tree.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Segment Tree Visualizer","x":"Module 03 · Data structures See exactly which O(log n) nodes a range query or point update touches."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/interactive/dp-table.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"DP Table Animator","x":"Module 05 · DP LCS, edit distance, and knapsack filled cell by cell, with dependencies and traceback."},{"u":"informatics-olympiad/interactive/flashcards.html","p":"💻 Informatics Olympiad · Interactive tool","h":"Flashcards","x":"All modules · Recall ~45 \"pattern → technique\" cards with spaced repetition."},{"u":"ielts/index.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Overview & roadmap","h":"IELTS Academic: Target Band 7.0–7.5","x":"Goal: an overall band 7.0–7.5 with no skill below 6.5 (a common university requirement; check yours). Time budget: ~8–10 weeks at 5–7 h/week, alongside the olympiads."},{"u":"ielts/index.html#the-test-at-a-glance","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Overview & roadmap","h":"The test at a glance","x":"Skill Time Format Band 7 needs (approx.) Listening ~30 min (+ transfer time on paper) 4 parts, 40 questions ~30/40 (7.5 ≈ 32/40) Reading (Academic) 60 min 3 long texts, 40 questions ~30/40 (7.5 ≈ 33/40) Writing 60 min Task 1 (≥150 words, data/process/map) + Task 2 (≥250 words, essay). Task 2 counts double. Band 7 on 4 criteria Speaking 11–14 min Part 1 interview · Part 2 a 2-min talk · Part 3 discussion Band 7 on 4 criteria Overall band = the average of the four, rounded to the nearest half band (x.25 → x.5, x.75 → next whole band). Raw-score conversions vary slightly between tests, so treat t"},{"u":"ielts/index.html#the-map","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Overview & roadmap","h":"The map","x":"Module Time Focus 01 Test strategy & scoring 2 h what examiners reward, the band 7 descriptors 02 Listening 1–2 wk prediction, signpost words, spelling, distractors 03 Reading 2 wk skim/scan, T/F/NG, matching headings, time control 04 Writing Task 1 1–2 wk overview, key features, comparison language 05 Writing Task 2 2–3 wk position, paragraph structure, idea development 06 Speaking 2–3 wk fluency, extending answers, Part 2 structure 07 Vocabulary & grammar for band 7 ongoing collocations, academic words, complex sentences Writing and Speaking are where most strong students lose marks. Give th"},{"u":"ielts/index.html#interactive-tools-interactive","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Overview & roadmap","h":"Interactive tools → interactive/","x":"- Band calculator: raw Listening/Reading scores → bands, and overall band rounding. - Writing practice: timed Task 1/Task 2 with a live word count, a prompt bank, and a band-7 self-check. - Speaking practice: random Part 1/2/3 questions, a 1-min prep + 2-min talk timer, and recording yourself (if your browser allows the microphone). - Flashcards: academic collocations, linking language, Task 1 trend vocabulary."},{"u":"ielts/index.html#10-week-plan","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Overview & roadmap","h":"10-week plan","x":"Weeks Focus 1 Diagnostic: one full official practice test, under real timing → find your weakest skill. Read module 01. 2–3 Listening + Reading technique (modules 02–03): 1 section a day, review every error. 3–6 Writing (modules 04–05): 2 Task 2 essays + 1 Task 1 per week. Self-check with the descriptors, and get feedback if you can. 4–8 Speaking (module 06): 15 min/day of out-loud practice, recording yourself 2–3×/week. all Vocabulary (module 07): 10 collocations/day in the flashcards. Read 1 Economist/Guardian/Scientific American article a day. 9–10 3–4 full mock tests under exam conditions."},{"u":"ielts/01-test-strategy.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","h":"01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","x":"Goal: know exactly what is graded so you practice the right things. · Time: ~2 h"},{"u":"ielts/01-test-strategy.html#what-examiners-grade","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","h":"What examiners grade","x":"Writing (each criterion is 25%): Criterion Band 7 in plain words Task Achievement (T1) / Task Response (T2) covers every part of the task. T1: a clear overview of the main trends. T2: a clear position throughout, with main ideas extended and supported Coherence & Cohesion logical organization, clear progression, a range of linking devices (not mechanical), one clear central idea per paragraph Lexical Resource enough range for flexibility and precision, less common vocabulary, awareness of style and collocation, only occasional errors Grammatical Range & Accuracy a variety of complex structures"},{"u":"ielts/01-test-strategy.html#general-strategy","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","h":"General strategy","x":"- Accuracy beats ambition. A simple sentence with no errors is better than a complex sentence that breaks. Band 7 needs frequent error-free sentences. - Answer the question asked. Off-topic content caps Task Response no matter how good the English is. - Never memorize whole answers. Examiners recognize them and penalize them. Memorize structures and collocations instead. - Computer vs paper: the content is the same. Computer-delivered has faster results and typing (it helps if you type quickly). Do your mocks in the same format you'll take. - One Skill Retake: some test centers let you retake "},{"u":"ielts/01-test-strategy.html#skip-test","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] You can name the 4 Writing criteria and 4 Speaking criteria, and say what band 7 needs in each. - [ ] You know how the overall band is rounded."},{"u":"ielts/02-listening.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 02 · Listening","h":"02 · Listening","x":"Goal: ≥ 30/40 consistently (band 7), 32+ for 7.5. · Time: 1–2 weeks, then maintenance"},{"u":"ielts/02-listening.html#the-4-parts","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 02 · Listening","h":"The 4 parts","x":"1. A conversation, everyday social context (form/note completion). 2. A monologue, everyday context (maps, MCQ, matching). 3. A conversation, academic context (2–4 speakers discussing an assignment). 4. A monologue, an academic lecture (note completion, the hardest). The audio plays once. Difficulty rises from Part 1 to Part 4."},{"u":"ielts/02-listening.html#core-techniques","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 02 · Listening","h":"Core techniques","x":"- Predict before listening: use the reading time to underline keywords, and predict the type of answer (number? noun? plural?) and grammar. - Follow the signposts: \"however\", \"actually\", \"but then\", \"the main reason\", \"finally\". Answers often follow a correction. - Distractors: speakers often say a wrong answer first and then correct it (\"Tuesday… no, sorry, Thursday\"). Wait for the final answer. - Paraphrase: the question rarely uses the same words as the audio. Train your ear for synonyms. - Spelling and word limits count: \"NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS\" means a 3-word answer is wrong. Practice spe"},{"u":"ielts/02-listening.html#practice-routine","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 02 · Listening","h":"Practice routine","x":"1. Do one section under test conditions. 2. Mark it. For every error, listen again with the transcript and label the cause: spelling / distractor / missed paraphrase / lost place / vocabulary. 3. Log the causes in progress.md. Fix the most frequent one first. 4. Daily ear training: 15 min of podcasts/lectures at natural speed (BBC 6 Minute English → TED → university lectures)."},{"u":"ielts/02-listening.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 02 · Listening","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books (official past-style tests) 💲, the newest volumes first. - IELTS official free practice tests. - British Council: IELTS preparation (free practice and the IELTS Ready course)."},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"03 · Reading (Academic)","x":"Goal: ≥ 30/40 (band 7), 33+ for 7.5. · Time: 2 weeks, then maintenance"},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html#format","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"Format","x":"3 long passages (journals, books, magazines; ~2,150–2,750 words total), 40 questions, 60 minutes, with no extra transfer time. Aim for ~17 / 20 / 23 min per passage, since passage 3 is the hardest."},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html#question-types-tactics","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"Question types & tactics","x":"Type Tactic True / False / Not Given (facts) · Yes / No / Not Given (writer's views) False = the text contradicts it. Not Given = the text says nothing about it. Don't use outside knowledge. Watch qualifiers: all/some/always/usually. Matching headings Read the headings first. Skim each paragraph's first and last sentences for its main idea, not a detail. Do it before the detail questions. Matching information (which paragraph contains…) Usually not in order and hardest. Do it last, after you know the text. Sentence / summary / note completion Respect the word limit. Answers usually come in tex"},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html#core-techniques","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"Core techniques","x":"- Skim (2–3 min): get the topic and paragraph structure. Scan: hunt for names, numbers, and keywords. - Paraphrase awareness again: \"decline\" ↔ \"fall\", \"a growing number of\" ↔ \"increasingly\". - Time discipline: never spend 2 min on one question. Mark it and return. - Read widely daily (academic register): The Economist, Scientific American, New Scientist, The Conversation. Your physics/math reading counts if it's in English!"},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html#practice-routine","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"Practice routine","x":"- Do one passage a day under time pressure. For each error, find the exact sentence in the text that gives the answer, and write why you chose wrong."},{"u":"ielts/03-reading.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 03 · Reading (Academic)","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books 💲 + official free practice. - IELTS Liz: Reading tips (free lessons per question type)."},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","x":"Goal: a reliable band 7 report in 20 minutes. · Time: 1–2 weeks 👉 Practice with the timer: Writing practice tool"},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html#the-task","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"The task","x":"Describe visual information (line/bar/pie chart, table, process, map, or a mix) in ≥ 150 words in ~20 min. Report what's there. No opinions, no causes you invent."},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html#the-4-paragraph-structure","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"The 4-paragraph structure","x":"1. Introduction (1 sentence): paraphrase the prompt. \"The line graph illustrates how … changed between 2000 and 2020.\" 2. Overview (2 sentences): the most important paragraph. The 2–3 biggest trends or features, with no numbers. \"Overall, X rose steadily, whereas Y declined. X was the highest throughout.\" Without a clear overview, you're capped at around band 5–6 on Task Achievement. 3. Body 1: details + data for the first group of features. 4. Body 2: details + data for the second group, with comparisons."},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html#language-toolkit","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"Language toolkit","x":"- Trends: rise / increase / grow / climb · fall / decrease / decline / drop · fluctuate · remain stable / level off · peak at · hit a low of. - Degree: dramatically, sharply, significantly · steadily, gradually · slightly, marginally. - Two patterns: \"X rose sharply\" (verb + adverb) ↔ \"There was a sharp rise in X\" (adjective + noun). Alternate them. - Comparison: twice as high as, considerably more than, the largest share, by contrast, whereas, while. - Approximation: just under a third, roughly half, approximately 40%. - Processes: the passive voice + sequencers. \"First, the beans are harvest"},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html#traps","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"Traps","x":"- Listing every number with no grouping or comparison. - Missing the overview, or putting numbers in it. - Copying phrases from the prompt (paraphrase!). Writing < 150 words (penalized)."},{"u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ IELTS Liz: Writing Task 1 (free model answers). - Cambridge IELTS books' model answers with examiner comments 💲."},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"05 · Writing Task 2","x":"Goal: band 7+ essays in 40 minutes. This is worth twice Task 1. · Time: 2–3 weeks, then 2 essays/week 👉 Practice with the timer: Writing practice tool"},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#essay-types-identify-the-type-first","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Essay types (identify the type first!)","x":"Type Prompt signal What you must do Opinion \"To what extent do you agree or disagree?\" A clear position in the intro, kept consistent to the end Discussion \"Discuss both views and give your own opinion.\" Both sides + your view Advantages / disadvantages \"Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?\" Both, plus which one outweighs Problem / solution (or cause / solution) \"What are the problems… What can be done?\" Answer both questions Two-part / direct questions Two separate questions Answer each one explicitly"},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#structure-45-paragraphs-270300-words","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Structure (4–5 paragraphs, ~270–300 words)","x":"1. Intro (2–3 sentences): paraphrase the topic + thesis (your position / what the essay will do). 2. Body 1 and Body 2: a topic sentence → explanation → example (specific, realistic) → link back to the question. One main idea per paragraph, developed deeply, beats three shallow ideas. 3. Conclusion (2 sentences): restate the position. No new ideas."},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#planning-5-min-the-step-that-decides-your-band","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Planning (5 min, the step that decides your band)","x":"- Underline the topic + the task. Decide your position. - Write 2 main ideas, each with its explanation and example. - Check: does every paragraph answer this question?"},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#band-7-moves","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Band 7 moves","x":"- Position throughout: the same view in the intro, the body topic sentences, and the conclusion. - Extend ideas: \"This is because…\", \"As a result…\", \"For instance, in countries such as…\". - Cohesion without overuse: vary the linkers, and use reference words (this trend, such policies). - Less common vocabulary used precisely: exacerbate, mitigate, a deterrent, disproportionately. Collocations matter more than rare words. - Complex grammar, accurately: conditionals, relative clauses, concessive clauses (Although…), passives, nominalization."},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#common-topics-build-idea-banks","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Common topics (build idea banks)","x":"Education · technology · environment · health · work · globalization · government spending · crime · media · cities · science & research (your strong area!)."},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#traps","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Traps","x":"- Memorized template intros (\"In this modern era…\"). Examiners discount them. - Sitting on the fence in an opinion essay. Under 250 words. Spending 50 min on Task 2 and rushing Task 1."},{"u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 05 · Writing Task 2","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ IELTS Liz: Writing Task 2 + IELTS Advantage (Chris Pell) essay guides. - ⭐ Feedback: have a teacher or a strong English user mark your essays against the descriptors. Without feedback, writing improves slowly. - Cambridge IELTS model essays with examiner comments 💲."},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"06 · Speaking","x":"Goal: band 7: speak at length, naturally, with flexible vocabulary. · Time: 15 min/day, 2–3 weeks focused 👉 Practice: Speaking practice tool (random questions, timers, recording)"},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html#the-3-parts","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"The 3 parts","x":"Part Time What happens How to answer 1 4–5 min Familiar topics (home, studies, hobbies…) 2–3 sentences: answer → reason → example/detail. Don't give one-word answers, and don't give speeches. 2 1 min prep + 1–2 min talk A cue card (\"Describe a time when…\") Speak the full 2 minutes. Use the prep minute to jot 4–5 keywords, not sentences. 3 4–5 min Abstract discussion linked to Part 2 Opinion → reason → example → (other side / future). Talk about society in general, not only you."},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html#part-2-structure-works-for-any-cue-card","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"Part 2 structure (works for any cue card)","x":"1. Set the scene: what/who/when/where (20 s). 2. The story/details: what happened, following the bullet points (60 s). 3. Feelings & reflection: why it mattered, how you feel now (30 s). 4. Filler you can always add: comparisons (\"Compared with…\"), a hypothetical (\"If I could do it again, I'd…\")."},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html#band-7-moves","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"Band 7 moves","x":"- Fluency over perfection. Self-correct quickly. Use natural fillers (\"Well…\", \"Let me think…\", \"That's an interesting question…\") rather than silence. - Extend: because… / for example… / on the other hand… / I suppose it depends on… - Vocabulary: topic-specific words + some idioms/phrasal verbs used naturally (\"I was over the moon\", \"it boils down to\"). Paraphrase when you don't know a word. - Grammar range: mix the tenses (past stories, present perfect experiences, conditionals for hypotheticals, future for plans). - Pronunciation: clear word stress, sentence stress, and intonation. Chunk yo"},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html#practice-routine","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"Practice routine","x":"- Daily: 3 random Part 1 questions + 1 Part 2 card (timed) + 2 Part 3 questions, out loud. - 2–3×/week: record a Part 2, listen back, and note 3 issues (hesitations, repeated words, grammar slips). Re-record once. - Weekly: a mock interview with a partner/teacher, ideally someone who'll interrupt and ask follow-ups."},{"u":"ielts/06-speaking.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 06 · Speaking","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ IELTS Liz: Speaking (question lists + model answers). - E2 IELTS and IELTS Advantage (YouTube speaking strategies + mock tests). - Official speaking test videos on the IELTS and British Council sites."},{"u":"ielts/07-vocab-grammar.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","h":"07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","x":"Goal: the language range band 7 requires, used accurately. · Time: 10–15 min/day, ongoing 👉 Drill: IELTS flashcards"},{"u":"ielts/07-vocab-grammar.html#vocabulary-learn-collocations-not-lists","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","h":"Vocabulary: learn collocations, not lists","x":"- Band 7's Lexical Resource rewards collocation and precision, not rare words. \"Make a decision\" (✓) vs \"do a decision\" (✗). - Academic Word List (AWL): ~570 word families that appear across academic texts (analyze, significant, factor, benefit, policy…). Learn them in phrases. - Topic banks: for each common Task 2 / Part 3 topic, 15–20 collocations, e.g. - Environment: curb emissions · renewable energy sources · carbon footprint · tackle climate change · sustainable development - Education: rote learning · critical thinking skills · tertiary education · a well-rounded curriculum - Technology:"},{"u":"ielts/07-vocab-grammar.html#grammar-range-accuracy","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","h":"Grammar: range + accuracy","x":"Structure Example Complex sentences (subordination) Although remote work offers flexibility, it can blur the line between work and home life. Relative clauses Students who study abroad often become more independent. Conditionals (1st, 2nd, 3rd, mixed) If governments invested more in public transport, congestion would fall. Passive voice The data were collected over a decade. (Task 1 processes) Nominalization The rapid expansion of cities has led to… (instead of \"cities expanded fast\") Cleft sentences What concerns many experts is the pace of change. Modal verbs for hedging This may/might/could"},{"u":"ielts/07-vocab-grammar.html#learn-from","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · 07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ English Collocations in Use (Advanced), McCarthy & O'Dell 💲. - ⭐ Advanced Grammar in Use, Hewings 💲 (or English Grammar in Use, Murphy, for gaps). - Academic Word List (Coxhead): the official list. - Oxford Learner's Dictionaries: example sentences and collocations. Ozdic: a free collocation dictionary."},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"IELTS Cheatsheet","x":"Skim this before every practice session and on test day."},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#score-targets-approx","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Score targets (approx.)","x":"Band Listening /40 Academic Reading /40 6.5 26–29 27–29 7.0 30–31 30–32 7.5 32–34 33–34 8.0 35–36 35–36 Overall = the average of 4, rounded to the nearest 0.5 (x.25 ↑ to x.5, x.75 ↑ to the next whole band)."},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#timing","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Timing","x":"Listening ~30 min · Reading 60 min (no transfer time: ~17/20/23 min) · Writing 20 min T1 + 40 min T2 · Speaking 11–14 min"},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#listening-reading","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Listening & Reading","x":"- Predict the answer type · watch for corrections (\"…no, actually…\") · respect the word limits · spelling counts - F = contradicts · NG = not mentioned · do matching headings first, matching information last"},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#writing-task-1-frame","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Writing Task 1 frame","x":"1. The [chart] illustrates/compares [what] in [where] between [when]. 2. Overall, [main trend 1], while [main trend 2]. [Biggest/smallest feature]. ← no numbers 3. Body 1: group A + data + comparison · 4. Body 2: group B + data + comparison Trend verbs: rise, climb, surge, decline, dip, plummet, fluctuate, plateau, peak at · adverbs: sharply, steadily, marginally"},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#writing-task-2-frame","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Writing Task 2 frame","x":"1. Intro: paraphrase + thesis (\"I strongly agree that… because…\") 2. Body: topic sentence → explain → example → link back (×2) 3. Conclusion: \"In conclusion, …\" restate the position + the main reasons Plan 5 min · write 30 min · check 5 min (articles, plurals, agreement, tense)"},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#linking-vary-them","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Linking (vary them!)","x":"Addition: Furthermore, Moreover, In addition · Contrast: However, Nevertheless, Whereas, Although · Cause/effect: Consequently, As a result, This leads to · Example: For instance, A case in point is · Concession: Admittedly, … Even so, …"},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#speaking","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Speaking","x":"- Part 1: answer + reason + example (2–3 sentences) - Part 2: scene → story → feelings → reflection. Talk the full 2 minutes. - Part 3: opinion → reason → example → other side/future - Buying time: \"That's an interesting question…\", \"I've never really thought about it, but…\" - Hedging: \"I'd say…\", \"It depends on…\", \"To some extent…\""},{"u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html#accuracy-checklist","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Cheatsheet","h":"Accuracy checklist","x":"articles · subject–verb agreement · uncountables (information, advice, research) · comma splices · prepositions · consistent tense · ≥150 / ≥250 words"},{"u":"ielts/resources.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"IELTS Resources","x":"⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free."},{"u":"ielts/resources.html#official-use-these-first-theyre-the-most-accurate","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"Official (use these first; they're the most accurate)","x":"- ⭐ IELTS official site (ielts.org): test format, free sample tests, and the public band descriptors for Writing and Speaking. - ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books (numbered volumes, each with 4 full tests, answer keys, transcripts, and examiner-marked model answers) 💲. The newest volumes are closest to the current test. - British Council: Take IELTS: free practice tests and the IELTS Ready preparation course. - IDP IELTS: free practice materials and webinars (IDP also runs tests in many countries)."},{"u":"ielts/resources.html#teachers-free-lessons","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"Teachers & free lessons","x":"- ⭐ IELTS Liz: free lessons for every question type, model answers, and topic lists. - IELTS Advantage (Chris Pell): writing structures and band-7+ strategy. - E2 IELTS (YouTube): test walkthroughs and speaking mocks."},{"u":"ielts/resources.html#language-building","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"Language building","x":"- English Collocations in Use: Advanced 💲 · Advanced Grammar in Use 💲 · Academic Word List. - Oxford Learner's Dictionaries · Ozdic collocations."},{"u":"ielts/resources.html#daily-input-reading-listening","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"Daily input (Reading & Listening)","x":"- Read: The Economist, The Guardian, Scientific American, New Scientist, The Conversation. - Listen: BBC Learning English (6 Minute English), BBC In Our Time, TED talks, university lecture podcasts."},{"u":"ielts/resources.html#practice-partners-feedback","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Resources","h":"Practice partners & feedback","x":"- A teacher or a study partner for Writing feedback and Speaking mocks. This matters more than any book. - This repo's interactive tools: band calculator, writing timer, speaking practice, flashcards."},{"u":"ielts/progress.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"IELTS: Progress & Log","x":""},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#key-dates","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Key dates","x":"Item Date University application deadlines (IELTS needed by) Test booked Backup / retake date"},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#target","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Target","x":"Overall 7.0–7.5, no skill below 6.5 (check your universities' exact requirements)."},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#mock-test-log","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Mock test log","x":"Date Source L raw → band R raw → band W (est.) S (est.) Overall Weakest diagnostic"},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#error-log-listeningreading","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Error log (Listening/Reading)","x":"Date Skill Question type Cause (spelling / distractor / paraphrase / time / vocab)"},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#writing-log","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Writing log","x":"Date Task Prompt (short) Words Time TR CC LR GRA Main fix for next time T2"},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#speaking-log","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Speaking log","x":"Date Part Topic Recorded? 3 issues heard 2"},{"u":"ielts/progress.html#vocabulary-log-phrase-example","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Progress log","h":"Vocabulary log (phrase + example)","x":"Phrase Example sentence Topic curb emissions Governments must curb emissions from heavy industry. environment"},{"u":"ielts/interactive/band-calculator.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Interactive tool","h":"Band Calculator","x":"Module 01 · Scoring Raw scores → bands, official overall rounding, and what you need to hit your target."},{"u":"ielts/interactive/writing-practice.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Interactive tool","h":"Writing Practice","x":"Modules 04–05 · Writing Random Task 1 charts and Task 2 prompts, a timer, a word count, and a band-7 checklist."},{"u":"ielts/interactive/speaking-practice.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Interactive tool","h":"Speaking Practice","x":"Module 06 · Speaking Random Part 1/2/3 questions, a prep + talk timer with beeps, and recording yourself."},{"u":"ielts/interactive/flashcards.html","p":"🗣️ IELTS Academic · Interactive tool","h":"Flashcards","x":"Module 07 · Vocabulary ~40 collocation, linking, paraphrase, and grammar cards with spaced repetition."},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Deep Learning: Beginner → Advanced","x":"Profile this is tuned for: high-school senior, strong in math and physics, basic Python and C++ STL, highly self-directed. What that means for the plan: - Math is a strength, so the math stays short. Module 01 covers only the DL-specific math you probably haven't seen yet: matrix calculus, probability for ML, and information theory. It skips the high-school parts. - Physics intuition will pay off. Gradient descent is a ball rolling on a potential. Softmax is the Boltzmann distribution. Diffusion models run Langevin dynamics. Energy-based models, Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, and physics-informed ne"},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#the-map","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"The map","x":"PHASE 1 · FOUNDATIONS PHASE 2 · CORE DL PHASE 3 · MODERN DL PHASE 4 · ADVANCED ┌──────────────────────┐ ┌──────────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────┐ │ 01 Math for DL │──▶│ 04 Neural nets (scratch) │──▶│ 08 Transformers │──▶│ 12 Frontiers: │ │ 02 Python + NumPy │ │ 05 PyTorch + training │ │ 09 LLMs │ │ theory, SSL, GNNs, │ │ 03 ML fundamentals │ │ 06 CNNs + vision │ │ 10 Generative models │ │ interpretability, │ └──────────────────────┘ │ 07 Sequences + RNNs │ │ 11 Reinforcement learn. │ │ sci-ML, GPU/systems │ └──────────────────────────┘ └────────────────"},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#fast-track-if-you-want-to-see-a-gpt-in-under-a-month","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Fast track (if you want to see a GPT in under a month)","x":"01 (matrix calculus section only) → 04 → 05 (first half) → 08. Come back for the rest later."},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#interactive-tools-interactive","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Interactive tools → interactive/","x":"- Optimizer landscape: SGD vs Momentum vs Adam on 2D loss surfaces (modules 01, 05). - Neural net playground: an MLP trained live with from-scratch backprop. Decision boundaries, overfitting, vanishing gradients (modules 03–05). - Attention & sampling: drag q/k vectors, see the √d scaling and the causal mask, try temperature/top-k/top-p (modules 08–09). - Flashcards: ~50 core facts with spaced repetition."},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#support-files","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Support files","x":"- cheatsheet.md: every key formula on one page. Use it for recaps. - resources.md: the full curated list, plus a paper reading list in order. - progress.md: checklists and your learning log. Update it every session."},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#hardware","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Hardware","x":"- You don't need a GPU to start. Modules 01–07 run on a laptop CPU. - For 08 and beyond, use free GPUs: Google Colab and Kaggle Notebooks (~30 GPU-h/week free). Rent a cloud GPU only for the large projects."},{"u":"deep-learning/index.html#study-protocol-per-module","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Overview & roadmap","h":"Study protocol (per module)","x":"1. Take the Skip test (5 min). Can you do it all? Skip to the next module. 2. Read Core ideas (10 min) so you know where you're headed. 3. Work through the ⭐ resource. Code along. Don't just watch. 4. Do the Build. Get stuck, then fix it yourself. That's where the learning happens. 5. Re-take the skip test. Write a log entry in progress.md. 6. Spaced review: skim Core ideas again after 1 week and after 1 month (≈5 min each)."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"01 · Math for Deep Learning","x":"Goal: the math DL actually uses and nothing more. · Time: 15–25 h · Needs: high-school calculus You're strong at math, so this module skips the basics. It covers three things school usually doesn't: linear algebra as geometry, matrix calculus, and probability/information theory for ML."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Explain matrix multiplication as a composition of linear maps, and what eigenvectors/SVD mean geometrically. - [ ] For f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^\\top A \\mathbf{x}, give \\nabla f. For L = \\ X\\mathbf{w}-\\mathbf{y}\\ ^2, give \\nabla{\\mathbf w} L and solve \\nabla L = 0. - [ ] Explain the chain rule for a composition f(g(h(x))) with vectors, using Jacobians. - [ ] Derive the MLE of a Gaussian's mean. Explain why minimizing MSE = maximizing Gaussian likelihood. - [ ] Define entropy, cross-entropy, and KL divergence, and say why D{KL} \\ge 0. - [ ] Explain why gradient descent with too large a s"},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Core ideas","x":""},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#linear-algebra-geometry-first","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Linear algebra (geometry first)","x":"- Vectors are data points. Matrices are linear maps, and AB means \"apply B, then A\". - Dot product \\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b = \\ \\mathbf a\\ \\ \\mathbf b\\ \\cos\\theta measures similarity. It's the core of attention. - Rank = dimension of the output space. Low-rank ideas drive LoRA and compression. - Eigen-decomposition A = Q\\Lambda Q^\\top (symmetric A): the axes the map only stretches. - SVD A = U\\Sigma V^\\top (any A): rotate → scale → rotate. This underlies PCA, low-rank approximation, and conditioning. - Norms: \\ \\cdot\\ 2 (weight decay), \\ \\cdot\\ 1 (sparsity), Frobenius norm for matrices. - Shape"},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#calculus-matrix-calculus","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Calculus → matrix calculus","x":"- Gradient \\nabla f points uphill. It is the steepest-ascent direction, perpendicular to level sets. - Jacobian J{ij} = \\partial yi / \\partial xj. Chain rule: J{f\\circ g} = Jf \\, Jg. Backprop = the chain rule applied right-to-left (vector-Jacobian products). - Identities worth memorizing: - \\nabla{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf a^\\top\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a - \\nabla{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x) = (A+A^\\top)\\mathbf x - \\nabla{\\mathbf w}\\ X\\mathbf w-\\mathbf y\\ ^2 = 2X^\\top(X\\mathbf w-\\mathbf y) - Shape trick: the gradient of a scalar w.r.t. a matrix has the same shape as that matrix. Use this to che"},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#optimization","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Optimization","x":"- Gradient descent: \\theta \\leftarrow \\theta - \\eta\\nabla L. On a quadratic it's stable only if \\eta < 2/\\lambda{\\max}. - 🔭 Physics: GD is an overdamped particle in potential L. Momentum is a heavy ball with friction. SGD noise acts like temperature. - Stochastic GD: gradients estimated on mini-batches. Noisy but cheap, and the noise even helps generalization. - Lagrange multipliers show up in constrained derivations (e.g. max-entropy → softmax, SVMs)."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#probability-statistics","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Probability & statistics","x":"- Random variables, expectation, variance. Linearity: \\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\mathbb E X+b\\mathbb E Y. - Bayes: p(\\theta\\mid D) \\propto p(D\\mid\\theta)\\,p(\\theta), i.e. posterior ∝ likelihood × prior. - Key distributions: Bernoulli/Categorical (classification), Gaussian (regression, noise, VAEs, diffusion). - Maximum likelihood (MLE): \\theta^ = \\arg\\max \\sumi \\log p(xi\\mid\\theta). Almost every DL loss is a negative log-likelihood: - Gaussian likelihood → MSE. Bernoulli/Categorical → cross-entropy. - MAP = MLE + prior. A Gaussian prior is exactly L2 weight decay. - Monte Carlo: \\mathbb Ep[f] \\approx "},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#information-theory","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Information theory","x":"- Entropy H(p) = -\\sum p\\log p: average surprise. 🔭 Same form as Gibbs entropy. - Cross-entropy H(p,q) = -\\sum p\\log q: the classification loss. - KL divergence D{KL}(p\\ q)=\\sum p\\log\\frac{p}{q} = H(p,q)-H(p) \\ge 0 (Jensen). It's asymmetric. - Softmax \\sigma(\\mathbf z)i = e^{zi}/\\sumj e^{zj}. 🔭 This is the Boltzmann distribution with energy -zi and temperature 1. The \"temperature\" in LLM sampling is literally T."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning, Deisenroth, Faisal & Ong (free PDF). Read ch. 2–7 selectively. Skim what you know. - ⭐ The Matrix Calculus You Need for Deep Learning, Parr & Howard. A short article that's essential. - 3Blue1Brown: Essence of Linear Algebra: watch it once for geometric intuition (~3 h). - Optional depth: MIT 18.06 (Strang), Harvard Stat 110 (Blitzstein), MIT 18.S096 Matrix Calculus for ML."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Build","x":"1. Derive \\nabla{\\mathbf w} for linear regression by hand. Then check it numerically with finite differences \\frac{f(w+h)-f(w-h)}{2h} in NumPy. 2. Write PCA with np.linalg.svd on a toy 2D dataset and plot the principal axes. 3. Simulate GD on f(x,y)=x^2+10y^2 with different \\eta. Watch the oscillation and divergence, and relate it to the condition number."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Traps","x":"- Memorizing formulas without checking shapes → derive with shapes, then verify numerically. - Over-studying math up front. Learn the rest just in time when a later module needs it."},{"u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 01 · Math for Deep Learning","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Every skip-test item is answerable · [ ] gradient check passes to ~1e-6 relative error"},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","x":"Goal: fluent, vectorized numeric Python and a working dev setup. · Time: 8–12 h · Needs: basic Python"},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Explain NumPy broadcasting and predict the shape of (32,1,5) + (4,5). - [ ] Compute pairwise distances between two point sets A (n,d), B (m,d) with no Python loop. - [ ] Use list/dict comprehensions, classes with call, generators, and type hints. - [ ] Create a virtual env, install packages, and run a Jupyter notebook. - [ ] git commit, branch, push. Write a .gitignore."},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Vectorize everything. A Python loop over 1M elements is ~100× slower than a NumPy op. Thinking in whole arrays is the core DL skill, and PyTorch works the same way. - Broadcasting rule: align shapes from the right. Each dim must be equal or 1. Dims of size 1 stretch. - Axis semantics: x.sum(axis=0) collapses rows. Use keepdims=True to keep shapes broadcastable. - Views vs copies: slicing gives a view (shared memory), fancy indexing gives a copy. reshape vs transpose + contiguity matters again in PyTorch. - dtype matters: float32 is the default in DL. float64 is for gradient checks. float16/b"},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ NumPy: the absolute basics + Broadcasting (official docs) - ⭐ CS231n Python/NumPy tutorial: short and DL-oriented. - From Python to NumPy, N. Rougier (vectorization mindset). - 100 NumPy exercises: do ~40 for fluency. - The Missing Semester (MIT): shell, git, editors. Do the git + shell lectures. - Plotting: Matplotlib quick start."},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Build","x":"1. Vectorized k-nearest-neighbors classifier on MNIST or CIFAR-10 (CS231n assignment 1 style). Time the loop version against the vectorized one. 2. Conway's Game of Life with NumPy only (convolution-like neighbor counts, no loops over cells)."},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Traps","x":"- Silent broadcasting bugs: (N,) - (N,1) becomes (N,N). Assert shapes in your code. - Living in notebooks forever. Move reusable code into .py files early."},{"u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] k-NN runs fully vectorized · [ ] project lives in a git repo with a README"},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","x":"Goal: the concepts every DL model inherits: loss, generalization, evaluation. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 01, 02"},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Explain train / validation / test splits and why tuning on the test set is cheating. - [ ] Derive the logistic regression gradient. Explain why its loss is cross-entropy. - [ ] Explain the bias–variance trade-off, and how L2 regularization changes it. - [ ] For an imbalanced dataset (1% positive), explain why accuracy is misleading, and define precision, recall, F1, and ROC-AUC. - [ ] Explain k-fold cross-validation and data leakage, with an example."},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Learning = optimization + generalization. Fit training data (minimize empirical risk) and do well on unseen data. The second is the hard part. - Supervised setup: data (xi, yi), model f\\theta, loss \\ell, minimize \\frac1N\\sum\\ell(f\\theta(xi), yi). - Linear regression: \\hat y = \\mathbf w^\\top\\mathbf x + b, MSE loss, closed form \\mathbf w = (X^\\top X)^{-1}X^\\top\\mathbf y. - Logistic regression: p(y{=}1\\mid\\mathbf x) = \\sigma(\\mathbf w^\\top\\mathbf x), binary cross-entropy loss, gradient X^\\top(\\hat{\\mathbf y}-\\mathbf y). A neural net is stacked logistic regressions. - Softmax regression: the mul"},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Machine Learning Specialization (Andrew Ng, Coursera): courses 1–2. Audit for free. You'll move fast. Or, if you prefer reading: - ⭐ (alt) An Introduction to Statistical Learning, with Python (ISLP): free PDF, ch. 2–6, 8, 10, 12. - Stanford CS229 lecture notes: the math-heavy version, which suits you well (GLMs, the exponential family). - scikit-learn user guide: the practical API and a great reference. - Google ML Crash Course: quick, interactive refresher."},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Build","x":"1. Logistic regression from scratch in NumPy (gradient descent, no sklearn) on a binary dataset. Plot the loss curve and the decision boundary. 2. Same dataset with sklearn: compare against yours, then run 5-fold cross-validation and a grid search over the regularization strength. 3. A Kaggle \"Getting Started\" competition (Titanic or House Prices): full pipeline with a gradient-boosting baseline."},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Traps","x":"- Celebrating 99% accuracy on a 99%-majority dataset. - Fitting the scaler on all the data before splitting (leakage). - Skipping baselines. Always compare against a trivial baseline (predict the mean or the majority class)."},{"u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Scratch and sklearn logistic regression agree · [ ] you can explain every number on your confusion matrix"},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","x":"Goal: understand backprop so well you could build PyTorch's core yourself. · Time: 15–20 h · Needs: 01, 03 The most important module. Everything after this is architecture choices on top of the same engine."},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Draw a 2-layer MLP. Write the forward pass in matrix form with shapes. - [ ] Derive backprop for Linear → ReLU → Linear → softmax-cross-entropy by hand. - [ ] Show that \\partial L/\\partial \\mathbf z = \\hat{\\mathbf y} - \\mathbf y for softmax + cross-entropy. - [ ] Explain why stacked linear layers without nonlinearities collapse to one linear layer. - [ ] Explain reverse-mode autodiff and why it suits \"many params → one scalar loss\". - [ ] Explain vanishing gradients with sigmoid, and why ReLU helps."},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Neuron: a = \\phi(\\mathbf w^\\top\\mathbf x + b). Layer: \\mathbf h = \\phi(W\\mathbf x + \\mathbf b). MLP: a stack of layers. - Why nonlinearity: W2(W1\\mathbf x) = (W2W1)\\mathbf x is still linear. \\phi (ReLU, GELU, tanh) gives expressive power. - Universal approximation: one wide hidden layer can approximate any continuous function. Depth makes it efficient (exponentially fewer units for compositional functions). - Computational graph: each op is a node. Forward computes values. Backward sends gradients from the loss back to every parameter. - Backprop = chain rule + caching. Each node only needs "},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Neural Networks: Zero to Hero (Andrej Karpathy): videos 1–5 (micrograd → makemore with MLPs, BatchNorm, \"Becoming a Backprop Ninja\"). Code along with every line. This is the best single resource in the whole curriculum. - 3Blue1Brown: Neural Networks: the intuition, watched first (~1.5 h). - Neural Networks and Deep Learning (Michael Nielsen): free book. Ch. 1–3 for a clear backprop derivation. - CS231n: Backprop notes + Yes you should understand backprop."},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Build","x":"1. micrograd: a scalar autograd engine (~150 lines) plus a tiny MLP that learns a 2D dataset (moons). Follow Karpathy, then rewrite it from memory. 2. NumPy MLP on MNIST with hand-derived matrix backprop. Target ≥ 97% test accuracy. Gradient-check every layer. 3. 💡 C++ bonus: port micrograd to C++ (a Value class with sharedptr children and a topological sort). It cements the graph idea and uses your STL skills."},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Traps","x":"- \"I understand it from the video.\" You don't until you've written it from a blank file. - Forgetting to zero gradients between steps (they accumulate). - Using float32 for gradient checks. Use float64."},{"u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] micrograd rewritten from memory · [ ] NumPy MLP ≥ 97% on MNIST · [ ] all skip-test derivations done on paper"},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","x":"Goal: train any model reliably, and know what to do when it doesn't train. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 04"},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Write a full training loop from memory: Dataset/DataLoader, nn.Module, loss, optimizer, zerograd → backward → step, eval with torch.nograd() + model.eval(). - [ ] Explain SGD + momentum vs Adam vs AdamW (and why decoupled weight decay matters). - [ ] Explain what BatchNorm and LayerNorm normalize over, and the train/eval difference for BatchNorm. - [ ] Explain why a LR warmup + cosine decay schedule is standard. - [ ] Your loss is NaN at step 200. List five things to check. - [ ] Explain mixed precision (bf16/fp16) and gradient clipping."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Core ideas","x":""},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#pytorch-mechanics","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"PyTorch mechanics","x":"- Tensor = a NumPy array + device (CPU/GPU) + autograd. requiresgrad=True records ops into a graph. - loss.backward() fills .grad. The optimizer reads .grad to update parameters. optimizer.zerograd() resets them. - nn.Module: define layers in init and compute in forward. Parameters register automatically. - Dataset / DataLoader: batching, shuffling, worker processes. - Modes: model.train() / model.eval() switch dropout and BatchNorm behavior. torch.nograd() turns off graph building. - Devices: move the model and the data .to(device). Most \"device mismatch\" errors come from this. - torch.compil"},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#optimizers","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Optimizers","x":"- SGD + momentum: v \\leftarrow \\beta v + g,\\ \\theta \\leftarrow \\theta - \\eta v (heavy ball 🔭). - Adam: per-parameter adaptive LR from running averages of g (1st moment) and g^2 (2nd moment), with bias correction. - AdamW: Adam with weight decay applied directly to the weights, not via the gradient. The default for transformers. - LR is the 1 hyperparameter. Use warmup (stability early) + cosine/linear decay. LR range test to find a good value. - Batch size: larger means less noise. Scale the LR roughly linearly (SGD) or √ (Adam) with batch size."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#normalization-regularization","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Normalization & regularization","x":"- BatchNorm: normalize each feature over the batch. It uses running statistics at eval time. Common in CNNs. - LayerNorm / RMSNorm: normalize over the features of each example. Independent of batch size. Used in transformers. - Residual connections \\mathbf x + F(\\mathbf x) give gradients a highway. This is what makes deep nets trainable. - Dropout: randomly zero activations in training, which acts like an ensemble. Weight decay, data augmentation, early stopping, label smoothing."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#debugging-recipe-from-karpathys-recipe","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Debugging recipe (from Karpathy's \"Recipe\")","x":"1. Get to know the data: look at samples and label distributions. 2. Build a dumb baseline, fix random seeds, check the initial loss (≈ \\ln(C) for C classes). 3. Overfit a single batch to ~0 loss. If you can't, there's a bug. 4. Then add regularization and scale up. Change one thing at a time. 5. Track everything: loss curves (train and val), LR, gradient norms. Use Weights & Biases or TensorBoard."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#performance","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Performance","x":"- Mixed precision (torch.autocast, prefer bf16) gives ~2× speed and half the memory. - Gradient clipping (clip by norm, e.g. 1.0) prevents explosions. - Gradient accumulation simulates a large batch on a small GPU. - Checkpointing: save model + optimizer + step so you can resume."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Dive into Deep Learning (d2l.ai): free, PyTorch, math + code. Ch. 2–5, 8 (modern CNNs), 12 (optimization). Use it as your main textbook for modules 05–07. - ⭐ A Recipe for Training Neural Networks (Karpathy): read twice. - Understanding Deep Learning (Simon Prince, 2023): free, beautifully illustrated, and strong on theory. Ch. 6–9 (training, init, regularization). Your best theory companion. - PyTorch: Learn the Basics + Zero to Mastery PyTorch. - Deep Learning Tuning Playbook (Google): a systematic way to tune hyperparameters. - Alt. top-down style: fast.ai Practical Deep Learning. Great"},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Build","x":"1. Re-implement your MNIST MLP in PyTorch. Compare it to your NumPy version. 2. Fashion-MNIST/CIFAR-10 ablation study: run SGD vs Adam vs AdamW, ± BatchNorm, ± dropout, three LR schedules. Plot everything in W&B and write a one-page conclusion. 3. Deliberately break training (huge LR, no normalization, bad init, forgetting zerograd) and record what each failure looks like on the curves."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Traps","x":"- Forgetting model.eval() → BatchNorm/dropout are wrong at test time. - Calling .item() or moving tensors to the CPU every step slows everything down. - Tuning ten things at once. You learn nothing from that."},{"u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Training loop written from memory · [ ] ablation report done · [ ] you recognize failure modes from the loss curves alone"},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","x":"Goal: understand convolution, the classic architectures, and modern vision. · Time: 20–25 h · Needs: 05"},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Compute the output size of a conv: input 32, kernel 5, stride 2, padding 2. - [ ] Count the parameters of Conv2d(64, 128, kernelsize=3) and explain why it's tiny next to a dense layer. - [ ] Explain translation equivariance, weight sharing, and receptive field. - [ ] Explain why ResNet's skip connections made 100+ layer nets trainable. - [ ] Explain transfer learning: when to freeze, when to fine-tune. - [ ] Explain how a Vision Transformer (ViT) turns an image into tokens."},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Convolution = sliding the same small filter everywhere. This builds in the prior that local patterns matter anywhere in the image. - Output size: \\lfloor (n + 2p - k)/s \\rfloor + 1. Params: C{in}\\cdot C{out}\\cdot k^2 + C{out}. - 🔭 A convolution is the discrete version of what you know from Green's functions and signal filtering. In DL it's technically cross-correlation. - Pooling / strided conv downsample, so the receptive field grows. Feature hierarchy: edges → textures → parts → objects. - Architecture timeline (know the idea of each): - LeNet (1998): conv + pool. AlexNet (2012): ReLU, GP"},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Stanford CS231n: course notes + lecture videos (YouTube) + assignments 1–2. A gold standard. - d2l.ai ch. 7–8 (CNNs, modern CNNs) and ch. 14 (computer vision). - CNN Explainer (interactive): see a conv net run in your browser. - Feature Visualization (Distill): what CNN neurons actually detect. - PyTorch Image Models (timm): pretrained models for transfer learning."},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Build","x":"1. ResNet-18 from scratch on CIFAR-10. Target ≥ 93% with augmentation and a good LR schedule. 2. Transfer learning: fine-tune a pretrained model on a small custom dataset (e.g. 5 classes, ~100 photos each). Compare frozen vs full fine-tuning. 3. Visualize filters, feature maps, and a saliency map / Grad-CAM for your model's predictions. 4. Stretch: a small U-Net for segmentation (e.g. Oxford-IIIT Pets masks)."},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Traps","x":"- Off-by-one shape errors. Print shapes after every block while developing. - Forgetting ImageNet normalization (mean/std) when using a pretrained model. - Evaluating with train-time augmentation turned on."},{"u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] ResNet on CIFAR-10 ≥ 93% · [ ] fine-tuned model works on your own photos"},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","x":"Goal: learn how to model sequences, and why attention replaced recurrence. · Time: 10–15 h · Needs: 05 This module is deliberately short. RNNs are mostly history now, but the problems they ran into explain every transformer design choice. (Modern state-space models such as Mamba bring recurrence back. See module 12.)"},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Explain tokenization and embeddings: why one-hot is bad and what an embedding table is. - [ ] Write the vanilla RNN update. Explain why gradients vanish or explode through time. - [ ] Explain how the LSTM gates (forget/input/output) fix this. - [ ] Explain the seq2seq encoder-decoder bottleneck and how attention removes it. - [ ] Explain teacher forcing and autoregressive generation."},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Language modeling = predicting the next token: p(x1..xT) = \\prodt p(xt\\mid x{<t}). This one objective powers GPT. - Tokens: characters, words, or subwords (BPE, see module 09). Embedding = a learned lookup table E \\in \\mathbb R^{V\\times d}, where similar meanings end up as nearby vectors. - word2vec (2013): learn embeddings by predicting context words. Famous vector arithmetic: king − man + woman ≈ queen. - Vanilla RNN: \\mathbf ht = \\tanh(Wh\\mathbf h{t-1} + Wx\\mathbf xt + \\mathbf b). BPTT multiplies by Wh T times, so gradients scale like \\lambda{\\max}^T → vanish or explode. - LSTM / GRU: an "},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Karpathy Zero to Hero: makemore series: you'll already be partway through it from module 04. It builds bigram → MLP → WaveNet-style LMs. - ⭐ Understanding LSTM Networks (colah): the classic diagrams. - The Unreasonable Effectiveness of RNNs (Karpathy). - Stanford CS224n: lectures on word vectors, RNNs, seq2seq + attention. - d2l.ai ch. 9–11 (RNNs, modern RNNs, attention)."},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"Build","x":"1. Character-level LM (makemore): bigram → MLP → small RNN/LSTM on a names or Shakespeare dataset. Compare samples and validation loss. 2. Train word2vec (skip-gram with negative sampling) on a small corpus. Show nearest neighbors and analogies. 3. Stretch: a seq2seq + attention model for date-format translation (\"3rd of May 2021\" → \"2021-05-03\"). Plot the attention heatmap."},{"u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Char-LM produces plausible samples · [ ] you can explain the path from RNN → attention → transformer"},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"08 · Transformers","x":"Goal: be able to write a GPT from a blank file and explain every line. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 05, 07"},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Write scaled dot-product attention with shapes, and explain the \\sqrt{dk}. - [ ] Explain causal masking and why GPT needs it. - [ ] Explain multi-head attention: why multiple heads, and how shapes reshape. - [ ] Draw one (pre-norm) transformer block: LN → attention → residual → LN → MLP → residual. - [ ] Explain why positional information is needed. Compare sinusoidal, learned, and RoPE. - [ ] Give the time and memory complexity of attention in sequence length T. - [ ] Explain encoder-only (BERT) vs decoder-only (GPT) vs encoder-decoder (T5)."},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Attention: \\text{Attention}(Q,K,V) = \\text{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{QK^\\top}{\\sqrt{dk}} + M\\right)V - Q = XWQ,\\ K = XWK,\\ V = XWV. Each token queries every other token's keys and takes a weighted mix of their values. - Why \\sqrt{dk}: for random unit-variance entries, \\mathbf q\\cdot\\mathbf k has variance dk. Dividing keeps the softmax out of its saturated (zero-gradient) regime. - Mask M: -\\infty above the diagonal = causal (can't see the future). This lets one forward pass train on all T next-token predictions at once. - Attention is a set operation: permutation-equivariant, with no built-in no"},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out (Karpathy) + nanoGPT / build-nanogpt (the \"Let's reproduce GPT-2 (124M)\" video). - ⭐ The Illustrated Transformer (Jay Alammar): read before the video. - Attention Is All You Need (2017): read the paper after building it. It'll be easy by then. - The Annotated Transformer (Harvard NLP): the paper line by line in PyTorch. - 3Blue1Brown: Transformers & Attention (chapters 5–7): superb visual intuition. - Transformer Explainer (interactive): GPT-2 running live in the browser. - Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 12."},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Build","x":"1. Single-head attention in NumPy, checked against torch.nn.functional.scaleddotproductattention. 2. nanoGPT from a blank file on Tiny Shakespeare (char-level). Then add: multi-head, pre-norm, dropout, weight tying, LR warmup + cosine, AdamW. 3. Swap learned positions for RoPE and compare validation loss. Try a longer context. 4. Stretch: reproduce GPT-2 124M on FineWeb-Edu following build-nanogpt (needs a rented GPU: roughly 1–2 h on 8×A100, or a day-plus on a single GPU)."},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Traps","x":"- Mixing up (B, T, C) and (B, heads, T, headdim) reshapes/transposes. Write the shapes in comments. - A causal mask applied after the softmax (wrong) instead of before (right). - Thinking you understand it after watching. Close the video and rebuild from nothing."},{"u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 08 · Transformers","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] GPT written from a blank file without referencing code · [ ] every skip-test item answered on paper"},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"09 · Large Language Models","x":"Goal: understand the full LLM pipeline (data → pretraining → post-training → inference → evaluation) and be able to do each step at small scale. · Time: 25–40 h · Needs: 08"},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Explain how BPE tokenization is trained, and give two LLM quirks it causes. - [ ] State what scaling laws predict, and the Chinchilla rule of thumb (~20 tokens per parameter). - [ ] Explain the pipeline: pretraining → SFT → preference tuning (RLHF / DPO) → (RL for reasoning). - [ ] Explain LoRA and why it cuts fine-tuning memory. - [ ] Explain the KV cache and why inference is memory-bandwidth-bound. - [ ] Explain RAG and when to use it instead of fine-tuning. - [ ] Explain why benchmark scores can mislead (contamination, saturation)."},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Core ideas","x":""},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#data-tokenization","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Data & tokenization","x":"- BPE: start from bytes and repeatedly merge the most frequent adjacent pair → a vocabulary of ~32k–200k tokens. - Quirks: odd arithmetic (numbers split strangely), letter-counting failures, weaker non-English efficiency, and the space-before-word trick. - Pretraining data: trillions of tokens of web text (filtered, deduplicated), code, books, math. Data quality data quantity (e.g. FineWeb-Edu)."},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#pretraining","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Pretraining","x":"- Next-token cross-entropy on a decoder-only transformer. Loss in nats; perplexity = e^{\\text{loss}}. - Scaling laws (Kaplan 2020, Chinchilla 2022): loss falls as a power law in parameters N, data D, and compute C \\approx 6ND FLOPs. Compute-optimal is about D \\approx 20N (modern models train far past this, because smaller models are cheaper to serve). - 🔭 Power laws, universality, and \"emergence\" debates resemble critical phenomena in statistical physics. - Architecture upgrades (Llama-style): RMSNorm, SwiGLU, RoPE, grouped-query attention (GQA), no biases. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE): each toke"},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#post-training-turning-a-base-model-into-an-assistant","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Post-training (turning a base model into an assistant)","x":"1. SFT (supervised fine-tuning): train on (instruction, good response) pairs. Mostly teaches format and behavior. 2. Preference tuning: - RLHF: train a reward model on human comparisons → optimize the policy with PPO plus a KL penalty to the SFT model (see module 11). - DPO: optimizes preferences directly with a classification-style loss, no separate reward model or RL loop. Simpler, and very common. 3. RL for reasoning: RL with verifiable rewards (math answers, unit tests) produces long chain-of-thought \"thinking\" models (e.g. GRPO, used by DeepSeek-R1). This is the research frontier. - Param"},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#inference","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Inference","x":"- Autoregressive decoding: one forward pass per token. The KV cache stores past keys and values so each step is O(T), not O(T^2). - It's bottlenecked by memory bandwidth (reading the weights + cache each token), not FLOPs. Hence quantization (int8/int4), speculative decoding, batching (vLLM, PagedAttention). - Sampling: temperature, top-k, top-p (nucleus). Low T = deterministic, high T = creative."},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#using-extending-llms","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Using & extending LLMs","x":"- Prompting: few-shot examples, chain-of-thought, structured outputs. - RAG: retrieve relevant documents (embeddings + vector search) and put them in the context. Use it for knowledge; use fine-tuning for behavior/style. - Agents & tool use: the LLM calls functions/tools in a loop (code execution, search). - Evaluation: perplexity; benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, SWE-bench…); LLM-as-judge; always build your own task-specific eval. Watch for test-set contamination. - Safety & alignment: jailbreaks, reward hacking, honesty, interpretability (module 12). This is a serious open problem worth c"},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Stanford CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch: lectures + assignments (tokenizer, transformer, systems, scaling, data, alignment). The deepest modern course. Do it after nanoGPT. - ⭐ Karpathy: Let's build the GPT Tokenizer · Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT · nanochat (a full ChatGPT-style pipeline, from tokenizer through SFT/RL to a web UI, in one small repo). - Hugging Face LLM Course: the transformers, datasets, and PEFT ecosystem in practice. - Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch), Sebastian Raschka (book + repo). - The Smol Training Playbook (HF) and The Ultra-Scale Playbook: "},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Build","x":"1. BPE tokenizer from scratch (train, encode, decode). Match GPT-2's tokenizer (tiktoken) output. 2. Fine-tune with LoRA a small open model (~0.5–3B, e.g. Qwen/Llama/SmolLM) on a custom instruction dataset in Colab. Evaluate before and after on your own eval set. 3. DPO on a small preference dataset with Hugging Face trl. Compare outputs with the SFT model. 4. RAG app: embed your own notes (e.g. this repo!), retrieve, answer questions with citations. 5. Stretch: implement a KV cache in your nanoGPT and measure the tokens/sec speedup. Run nanochat end to end."},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#traps","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Traps","x":"- Fine-tuning to add knowledge (use RAG) instead of behavior. - Trusting public benchmarks over your own evals. - Chasing every new model release. Fundamentals change slowly, so read papers selectively."},{"u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 09 · Large Language Models","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Tokenizer matches tiktoken · [ ] LoRA fine-tune shows a measurable gain on your eval · [ ] you can explain the whole ChatGPT pipeline end to end"},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"10 · Generative Models","x":"Goal: learn the four big ideas for modeling p(x): autoregressive, VAE, GAN, diffusion/flow. · Time: 25–35 h · Needs: 01, 05 🔭 This module is where physics pays off most: diffusion is Langevin dynamics, score matching is about gradients of log-densities, flow matching moves probability mass by ODEs (continuity equation), and energy-based models are Boltzmann distributions."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Derive the ELBO: \\log p(x) \\ge \\mathbb E{q(z x)}[\\log p(x z)] - D{KL}(q(z x)\\,\\ \\,p(z)). - [ ] Explain the reparameterization trick and why it's needed. - [ ] Write the GAN min-max objective. Explain mode collapse. - [ ] Describe the DDPM forward process q(xt\\mid x0) in closed form, and what the network predicts. - [ ] Explain classifier-free guidance. - [ ] Explain the idea of flow matching / rectified flow in one paragraph."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Core ideas","x":""},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#the-landscape","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"The landscape","x":"Family Idea Pros Cons Used in Autoregressive p(x)=\\prod p(xt\\mid x{<t}) exact likelihood, simple slow sequential sampling GPT, audio LMs VAE latent z, maximize ELBO fast, smooth latent space blurry samples latent space for Stable Diffusion GAN generator vs discriminator game sharp, fast sampling unstable, mode collapse StyleGAN, super-resolution Diffusion / flow learn to reverse a noising process top quality, stable training many sampling steps Stable Diffusion, image/video/audio models"},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#autoencoders-vaes","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Autoencoders & VAEs","x":"- Autoencoder: encoder → bottleneck z → decoder, trained to reconstruct. It compresses, but it isn't a generative model. - VAE: encoder outputs q\\phi(z\\mid x) = \\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2), prior p(z)=\\mathcal N(0,I). - Loss = −ELBO = reconstruction + KL. Reparameterize z = \\mu + \\sigma\\odot\\epsilon,\\ \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I) so gradients flow through the sampling step. - 🔭 The ELBO is the (negative) variational free energy from statistical mechanics."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#gans","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"GANs","x":"- \\minG\\maxD\\ \\mathbb E[\\log D(x)] + \\mathbb E[\\log(1 - D(G(z)))], a two-player game. - Problems: unstable training, mode collapse. Fixes: WGAN (Wasserstein distance), spectral norm, StyleGAN tricks. Mostly displaced by diffusion, but the ideas live on (adversarial losses in VAE decoders, distillation)."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#diffusion-models-ddpm","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Diffusion models (DDPM)","x":"- Forward: gradually add Gaussian noise, q(xt\\mid x0) = \\mathcal N(\\sqrt{\\bar\\alphat}\\,x0,\\ (1-\\bar\\alphat)I). So xt = \\sqrt{\\bar\\alphat}\\,x0 + \\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alphat}\\,\\epsilon. - Train: a network \\epsilon\\theta(xt, t) predicts the noise. Loss \\ \\epsilon - \\epsilon\\theta(xt,t)\\ ^2. Literally an MSE regression. - Sample: start from pure noise and denoise step by step. - Score view: \\epsilon\\theta \\propto -\\nablax \\log pt(x) (the score). Sampling = Langevin dynamics / a reverse-time SDE (Song et al.). 🔭 Fokker–Planck, Brownian motion. - Speedups: DDIM (deterministic ODE sampling, fewer steps), co"},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#flow-matching-rectified-flow-current-state-of-the-art","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Flow matching / rectified flow (current state of the art)","x":"- Learn a velocity field v\\theta(x,t) whose ODE \\dot x = v\\theta carries noise to data along (nearly) straight paths. With x0 = noise and x1 = data, interpolate linearly xt = (1-t)x0 + t x1 and regress v\\theta(xt,t) onto x1 - x0. That's again a plain MSE. - 🔭 A continuity equation \\partialt p + \\nabla\\cdot(p v) = 0 is transporting probability mass. Used in Stable Diffusion 3, Flux, and many video models."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#also-know","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Also know","x":"- Normalizing flows: invertible maps with exact likelihood via the change of variables \\log p(x) = \\log p(z) + \\log \\det \\partial z/\\partial x . - Energy-based models: p(x) \\propto e^{-E(x)} (🔭 Boltzmann). Hard to normalize, sampled with MCMC."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 14–18 (unsupervised, GANs, flows, VAEs, diffusion). The clearest unified treatment, with notebooks. - ⭐ MIT 6.S184: Introduction to Flow Matching and Diffusion Models: lecture notes + labs. Built on SDEs/ODEs, perfect for a physics mind. - Lilian Weng: What are Diffusion Models? · From Autoencoder to Beta-VAE · From GAN to WGAN. - Yang Song: Generative Modeling by Estimating Gradients of the Data Distribution: the score/SDE view. - Hugging Face Diffusion Models Course + the diffusers library. - Papers: VAE · GAN · DDPM · Score SDE · Latent Diffusion"},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Build","x":"1. VAE on MNIST: visualize the 2D latent space and interpolate between digits. 2. DCGAN on a small image dataset. Observe instabilities and mode collapse. 3. DDPM from scratch (small U-Net) on MNIST/Fashion-MNIST, then add class conditioning + classifier-free guidance. 4. Flow matching on 2D toy data (e.g. moons → spiral), then on MNIST. Compare sampling steps with DDPM. 5. Stretch: fine-tune Stable Diffusion with LoRA/DreamBooth on your own images using diffusers."},{"u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 10 · Generative Models","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] ELBO derived on paper · [ ] your DDPM generates recognizable digits · [ ] you can explain diffusion in both the noise-prediction and the score/SDE views"},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"11 · Reinforcement Learning","x":"Goal: learn the core ideas of RL, deep RL algorithms, and RL's role in modern LLMs. · Time: 25–35 h · Needs: 01, 05"},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html#skip-test","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"Skip test","x":"- [ ] Define an MDP (S, A, P, R, \\gamma), return, policy, V^\\pi, Q^\\pi. - [ ] Write the Bellman expectation and optimality equations. - [ ] Explain exploration vs exploitation and ε-greedy. - [ ] Explain Q-learning's update, and what DQN adds (replay buffer, target network). - [ ] Derive the policy gradient \\nabla J = \\mathbb E[\\nabla\\log\\pi\\theta(a s)\\,A(s,a)]. Explain why baselines reduce variance. - [ ] Explain PPO's clipped objective, and how RLHF/GRPO use it for LLMs."},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html#core-ideas","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"Core ideas","x":"- Setup: an agent observes state s, takes action a, gets reward r and next state s'. Goal: maximize the expected return Gt = \\sumk \\gamma^k r{t+k}. - Value functions: V^\\pi(s) = \\mathbb E\\pi[Gt\\mid s], Q^\\pi(s,a) = \\mathbb E\\pi[Gt\\mid s,a]. Advantage A = Q - V. - Bellman equation: Q^(s,a) = \\mathbb E[r + \\gamma\\max{a'}Q^(s',a')]. A recursive consistency condition. 🔭 Analogous to the principle of optimality / Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman. - Dynamic programming (known model): value iteration, policy iteration. - Model-free, value-based: - TD learning: bootstrap from your own estimate, V(s) \\leftarro"},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html#learn-from","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"Learn from","x":"- ⭐ Sutton & Barto: Reinforcement Learning, an Introduction (2nd ed.): free. Ch. 1–6, 9, 13. The foundational text. - ⭐ OpenAI Spinning Up in Deep RL: the bridge to deep RL. Read \"Introduction to RL\" parts 1–3, then the algorithm docs. - David Silver's RL course (UCL/DeepMind): classic lectures that pair with Sutton & Barto. - Hugging Face Deep RL Course: hands-on and free. - CleanRL: single-file, readable implementations to study. Gymnasium for environments. - The 37 Implementation Details of PPO: why your PPO doesn't work. - Advanced: UC Berkeley CS285 (Sergey Levine)."},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html#build","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"Build","x":"1. Tabular Q-learning on FrozenLake / CliffWalking. Visualize the value function. 2. DQN on CartPole (then Atari Pong if you have a GPU). 3. REINFORCE → actor-critic → PPO on CartPole/LunarLander, each written by you and compared against CleanRL. 4. Stretch: a GRPO-style RL fine-tune of a tiny LM on arithmetic problems with a verifiable reward (trl has a GRPOTrainer). 5. 🔭 Fun: train an agent on a physics environment (MuJoCo via Gymnasium: Hopper, Ant)."},{"u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html#done-when","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 11 · Reinforcement Learning","h":"Done when","x":"- [ ] Your PPO solves LunarLander · [ ] you can explain how RL is used to post-train LLMs"},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","x":"Goal: move from student to researcher/engineer: read papers, go deep in one area. · Time: open-ended · Needs: 08 (+ 10/11 for some tracks) There's no skip test here. Pick 1–2 tracks that excite you and go deep, and treat the rest as awareness. Each track lists the core ideas and the ⭐ entry point."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#how-to-read-papers-do-this-first","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"How to read papers (do this first)","x":"- Three passes (Keshav, \"How to Read a Paper\"): (1) title/abstract/figures/conclusion, 10 min → (2) the main method, skipping proofs, 1 h → (3) re-derive and re-implement it. - Reimplement reread. The best way to understand a paper is to reproduce its key result at small scale. - Find papers and code: arXiv (cs.LG, cs.CL, cs.CV), Hugging Face Papers (daily trending, with linked code), alphaXiv (discussion), and authors' blogs. - Keep a paper log in progress.md: one line per paper (problem → idea → result → my doubt)."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-a-deep-learning-theory-great-fit-for-a-mathphysics-mind","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track A · Deep learning theory 🔭 (great fit for a math/physics mind)","x":"- Optimization landscape: saddle points, flat vs sharp minima, edge of stability. - Generalization puzzles: over-parameterized nets fit random labels yet generalize; double descent; grokking; implicit bias of SGD. - Infinite-width limits: Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) (lazy regime ↔ kernel regression) vs μP / mean-field (feature learning). μP also lets you transfer hyperparameters across model sizes. - Scaling laws and statistical-physics approaches to learning. - ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 20 (\"Why does deep learning work?\") → The Principles of Deep Learning Theory (Roberts, Yai"},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-b-mechanistic-interpretability-ai-safety","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track B · Mechanistic interpretability & AI safety","x":"- Reverse-engineer how networks compute: circuits, induction heads, superposition (more features than neurons), sparse autoencoders (SAEs) to extract interpretable features, attribution graphs. - Safety: alignment, reward hacking, scalable oversight, evals for dangerous capabilities. - ⭐ ARENA curriculum (hands-on, free notebooks) + TransformerLens. - Read: Transformer Circuits Thread (A Mathematical Framework, Toy Models of Superposition, Scaling Monosemanticity), Neel Nanda's getting-started guide, BlueDot Impact courses."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-c-scientific-ml-physics-informed-dl","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track C · Scientific ML & physics-informed DL 🔭","x":"- PINNs: put the PDE residual in the loss. Neural operators (FNO, DeepONet): learn maps between function spaces. - Equivariant networks: build symmetries (rotation, translation, permutation) into the architecture → molecules, particle physics. Geometric deep learning. - Neural ODEs, Hamiltonian/Lagrangian neural networks (energy-conserving by construction). - Applications: AlphaFold (proteins), weather (GraphCast), ML for particle physics and cosmology, molecular dynamics potentials. - ⭐ Geometric Deep Learning (Bronstein et al.) (book + lectures) and Steve Brunton's Physics-Informed ML lectur"},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-d-ml-systems-gpus-efficiency-where-your-c-shines","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track D · ML systems, GPUs & efficiency 💻 (where your C++ shines)","x":"- GPU fundamentals: SMs, warps, memory hierarchy (HBM → L2 → shared memory/SRAM → registers), memory-bound vs compute-bound, roofline model, arithmetic intensity. - Kernels: write CUDA (C++) and Triton (Python) kernels: tiled matmul, softmax, fused ops, FlashAttention. - Distributed training: data parallel (DDP), FSDP/ZeRO, tensor and pipeline parallelism. Communication collectives (all-reduce). - Efficiency: quantization (int8/int4, GPTQ/AWQ), pruning, distillation, speculative decoding, torch.compile. - ⭐ Programming Massively Parallel Processors (Hwu, Kirk, El Hajj) + the GPU MODE lecture s"},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-e-self-supervised-multimodal-representation-learning","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track E · Self-supervised, multimodal & representation learning","x":"- Contrastive learning: SimCLR, MoCo. InfoNCE loss pulls positives together and pushes negatives apart. - Masked modeling: BERT (text), MAE (images). Self-distillation: DINO/DINOv2. JEPA (predict in latent space). - Multimodal: CLIP (image–text contrastive), vision-language models (LLaVA-style: vision encoder + projector + LLM), audio (Whisper), video. - ⭐ Lilian Weng: Contrastive Representation Learning + the CLIP paper. Course: CMU Multimodal ML."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-f-graphs-beyond-transformer-architectures","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track F · Graphs & beyond-transformer architectures","x":"- GNNs: message passing \\mathbf hv' = \\phi(\\mathbf hv, \\textstyle\\bigoplus{u\\in N(v)}\\psi(\\mathbf hu)). GCN, GAT, graph transformers. Used for molecules, social networks, physics simulation. - State-space models: S4 → Mamba (selective SSM, linear-time sequence modeling). Hybrids with attention. 🔭 Discretized linear ODEs. - ⭐ Stanford CS224W: ML with Graphs + PyTorch Geometric. For SSMs: The Annotated S4, the Mamba paper."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#track-g-other-frameworks-worth-knowing","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Track G · Other frameworks worth knowing","x":"- JAX (functional NumPy + grad/jit/vmap/pmap). Popular in research and on TPUs. Try it once you know PyTorch well: JAX docs."},{"u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html#becoming-a-contributor-high-school-senior-edition","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · 12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","h":"Becoming a contributor (high-school senior edition)","x":"- Portfolio: 3–5 polished GitHub projects with READMEs and results (e.g. GPT from scratch, a DDPM, a paper reproduction). These count more than certificates. - Competitions: the International Olympiad in AI (IOAI) is a science olympiad for high-school students, so it's worth checking whether your country runs a national selection. Also Kaggle and AIcrowd. - Write: blog your reimplementations. Teaching forces understanding (the Feynman technique). - Community: EleutherAI, GPU MODE, and Hugging Face Discords. Open-source contributions (docs, bug fixes) to transformers, diffusers, TransformerLens"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Deep Learning Cheatsheet","x":"Everything key on one page. Skim this to recap, and open the module (→ NN) if a line feels unfamiliar."},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#math-01","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Math (→ 01)","x":"Concept Formula Linear-regression gradient \\nabla{\\mathbf w}\\ X\\mathbf w-\\mathbf y\\ ^2 = 2X^\\top(X\\mathbf w-\\mathbf y) Quadratic form gradient \\nabla(\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x) = (A+A^\\top)\\mathbf x Chain rule (Jacobians) J{f\\circ g} = Jf Jg; backprop = vector-Jacobian products, right to left SVD A = U\\Sigma V^\\top (rotate · scale · rotate) GD stability (quadratic) \\eta < 2/\\lambda{\\max}(H) Bayes p(\\theta\\mid D)\\propto p(D\\mid\\theta)p(\\theta) Entropy / CE / KL H(p)=-\\sum p\\log p · H(p,q)=-\\sum p\\log q · D{KL}=H(p,q)-H(p)\\ge0 Softmax \\sigma(\\mathbf z)i = e^{zi/T}/\\sumj e^{zj/T} (Boltzmann, temp"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#ml-basics-03","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"ML basics (→ 03)","x":"- Logistic regression: \\hat y=\\sigma(\\mathbf w^\\top\\mathbf x), gradient X^\\top(\\hat{\\mathbf y}-\\mathbf y) - High train error → underfit (bigger model). Train ≪ val → overfit (data, regularization). - Precision =\\frac{TP}{TP+FP} · Recall =\\frac{TP}{TP+FN} · F1 = harmonic mean - Split: train (fit) / val (decide) / test (report once). Fit preprocessing on train only."},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#neural-nets-04","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Neural nets (→ 04)","x":"- Layer: \\mathbf h=\\phi(W\\mathbf x+\\mathbf b). Batch linear Y=XW^\\top: \\partial L/\\partial W = (\\partial L/\\partial Y)^\\top X, \\partial L/\\partial X = (\\partial L/\\partial Y)W - Softmax + CE: \\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf z = \\hat{\\mathbf y}-\\mathbf y - Init: Xavier \\text{Var}=1/n{in} (tanh) · He 2/n{in} (ReLU) - Initial loss sanity check: \\approx\\ln C for C balanced classes"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#training-05","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Training (→ 05)","x":"for x, y in loader: x, y = x.to(dev), y.to(dev) with torch.autocast(devicetype=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16): loss = lossfn(model(x), y) opt.zerograd(settonone=True) loss.backward() torch.nn.utils.clipgradnorm(model.parameters(), 1.0) opt.step(); sched.step() - Defaults that usually work: AdamW (β=(0.9, 0.95–0.999), wd 0.01–0.1), warmup + cosine, bf16, grad clip 1.0. - Debug order: check the data → check the initial loss → overfit one batch → scale up → change one thing at a time. - BatchNorm normalizes over the batch (CNNs) · LayerNorm/RMSNorm over the features (transformers)."},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#cnns-06","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"CNNs (→ 06)","x":"- Output size \\lfloor(n+2p-k)/s\\rfloor+1 · Params C{in}C{out}k^2+C{out} - ResNet: \\mathbf y=\\mathbf x+F(\\mathbf x) · Transfer: small data → freeze; more data → fine-tune all at a low LR."},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#sequences-07","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Sequences (→ 07)","x":"- LM: p(x{1:T})=\\prodt p(xt\\mid x{<t}) · Perplexity =e^{\\text{CE loss}} - RNN gradients scale like \\lambda^T → vanish/explode. LSTM's additive cell state fixes it."},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#transformers-08","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Transformers (→ 08)","x":"- \\text{Attn}(Q,K,V)=\\text{softmax}(QK^\\top/\\sqrt{dk}+M)V, causal mask M{ij}=-\\infty for ji - Block: x += attn(norm(x)); x += mlp(norm(x)), MLP d\\to4d\\to d - Params ≈ 12Ld^2 + Vd · Attention cost O(T^2d) · RoPE = relative position via rotation - Shapes: (B,T,C) → split heads (B,h,T,C/h) → attention → merge back"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#llms-09","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"LLMs (→ 09)","x":"- Training compute C\\approx 6ND FLOPs · Chinchilla-optimal D\\approx20N - Pipeline: pretrain → SFT → preference (RLHF-PPO / DPO) → RL with verifiable rewards (GRPO) - LoRA: W + BA, rank r\\ll d · KV cache: store past K,V → decoding is memory-bandwidth-bound - RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behavior"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#generative-10","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"Generative (→ 10)","x":"- ELBO: \\log p(x)\\ge\\mathbb Eq[\\log p(x z)]-D{KL}(q(z x)\\ p(z)) · Reparam: z=\\mu+\\sigma\\epsilon - GAN: \\minG\\maxD \\mathbb E[\\log D(x)]+\\mathbb E[\\log(1-D(G(z)))] - DDPM: xt=\\sqrt{\\bar\\alphat}x0+\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alphat}\\epsilon, loss \\ \\epsilon-\\epsilon\\theta(xt,t)\\ ^2 - CFG: \\hat\\epsilon=\\epsilonu+w(\\epsilonc-\\epsilonu) · Flow matching: regress v\\theta(xt,t) onto x1-x0"},{"u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html#rl-11","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Cheatsheet","h":"RL (→ 11)","x":"- Return Gt=\\sumk\\gamma^kr{t+k} · Bellman: Q^(s,a)=\\mathbb E[r+\\gamma\\max{a'}Q^(s',a')] - Q-learning: Q\\leftarrow Q+\\alpha[r+\\gamma\\max Q(s',\\cdot)-Q] - Policy gradient: \\nabla J=\\mathbb E[\\nabla\\log\\pi\\theta(a s)\\,A(s,a)] - PPO: \\mathbb E[\\min(rtAt,\\ \\text{clip}(rt,1\\pm\\epsilon)At)], rt=\\pi\\theta/\\pi{old}"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Resources: the curated master list","x":"Rule: ⭐ = the main resource, and the only one you need. Everything else is optional depth or an alternative explanation if the ⭐ doesn't click. All ⭐ items are free unless marked 💲."},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#the-core-stack-if-you-only-use-five-things","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"The core stack (if you only use five things)","x":"1. ⭐ Karpathy: Neural Networks Zero to Hero: build everything from scratch (modules 04, 07, 08, 09) 2. ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince, 2023): the theory textbook (all modules) 3. ⭐ Dive into Deep Learning (d2l.ai): the textbook with runnable PyTorch code (05–07) 4. ⭐ Stanford CS231n → CS336: university-grade assignments (06, 09) 5. ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning: math, only when you need it (01)"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#books-free-online-unless","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Books (free online unless 💲)","x":"Book Best for Understanding Deep Learning, Prince modern, clear, illustrated. Best overall DL textbook Dive into Deep Learning, Zhang et al. code + math side by side Mathematics for Machine Learning, Deisenroth et al. linear algebra, calculus, and probability for ML Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts, Bishop & Bishop rigorous, probabilistic view (free online viewing) Deep Learning, Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville classic (2016). Part I math and Part II are still great Probabilistic Machine Learning, Murphy encyclopedic reference (Book 1 + 2) Neural Networks and Deep Learning, Nielsen gentle"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#courses-lectures-free-on-youtube-or-the-course-site","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Courses (lectures free on YouTube or the course site)","x":"Course Module Notes 3Blue1Brown: linear algebra & neural nets 01, 04, 08 intuition first ML Specialization (Andrew Ng) 03 audit free Stanford CS229: Machine Learning 03 math-heavy fast.ai: Practical Deep Learning 05–06 top-down alternative Stanford CS231n: CNNs for Visual Recognition 06 assignments are gold Stanford CS224n: NLP with Deep Learning 07–08 Stanford CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch 09 hardest and best LLM course Hugging Face Learn (LLM, Diffusion, Deep RL courses) 09–11 practical ecosystem MIT 6.S184: Flow Matching & Diffusion 10 SDE/ODE view OpenAI Spinning Up 11 deep RL brid"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#blogs-interactive-explainers","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Blogs & interactive explainers","x":"- Lilian Weng: deep, well-cited surveys (diffusion, RL, LLMs, agents) - Andrej Karpathy's blog: Recipe for Training NNs, RNN effectiveness - colah's blog: LSTMs, visual explanations - Jay Alammar: the Illustrated Transformer / GPT-2 / BERT - Distill.pub: interactive research articles (archived, still excellent) - Transformer Circuits Thread: interpretability research - Sebastian Raschka's Ahead of AI: LLM research digests - Yang Song's blog: score-based generative models - Interactive: TensorFlow Playground · CNN Explainer · Transformer Explainer"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#code-to-read-well-written-references","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Code to read (well-written references)","x":"- micrograd · nanoGPT · build-nanogpt · nanochat · llm.c - CleanRL: single-file RL algorithms - timm: vision models · diffusers · transformers · trl - The Annotated Transformer · The Annotated S4"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#tools","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Tools","x":"- Compute: Google Colab, Kaggle Notebooks (free GPUs) · rent: Lambda, RunPod, Vast.ai - Tracking: Weights & Biases (free for personal use), TensorBoard - Data/models: Hugging Face Hub - Papers: arXiv, Hugging Face Papers, alphaXiv, Semantic Scholar"},{"u":"deep-learning/resources.html#paper-reading-order","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Resources","h":"Paper reading order","x":"Read each one after the module that covers it, when it'll feel easy. ✅ = reimplement a small version. Foundations 1. Learning representations by back-propagating errors (Rumelhart et al., 1986): backprop (just skim) 2. ImageNet Classification with Deep CNNs, AlexNet (2012) 3. Adam (2014) · Decoupled Weight Decay, AdamW (2017) 4. Dropout (2014) · Batch Normalization (2015) · Layer Normalization (2016) 5. ✅ Deep Residual Learning, ResNet (2015) Sequences → Transformers 6. word2vec (2013) · Seq2Seq (2014) · Bahdanau Attention (2014) 7. ✅ Attention Is All You Need (2017) 8. BERT (2018) · ✅ GPT-2 ("},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Progress & Learning Log","x":"Update this every session (2 minutes). It's your memory, so you never relearn what you've already covered."},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#status-board","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Status board","x":"Legend: ⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done · ⏭️ skipped (passed skip test) Module Status Skip test Build Started Finished 1-wk review 1-mo review 01 Math for DL ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 02 Python, NumPy & tools ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 03 ML fundamentals ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 04 Neural nets from scratch ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 05 PyTorch & training ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 06 CNNs & vision ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 07 Sequences & RNNs ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 08 Transformers ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 09 LLMs ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 10 Generative models ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 11 Reinforcement learning ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 12 Frontiers (track: ) ⬜ — ⬜"},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#projects-portfolio","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Projects portfolio","x":"Project Module Link to code Result micrograd (rewritten from memory) 04 NumPy MLP on MNIST 04 test acc: ResNet-18 on CIFAR-10 06 test acc: GPT from scratch 08 val loss: LoRA fine-tune 09 eval gain: DDPM / flow matching 10 samples: PPO on LunarLander 11 avg return:"},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#fuzzy-list","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Fuzzy list","x":"Things that didn't fully click. Review them at the start of the next session and cross them off when they make sense. - [ ] …"},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#paper-log","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Paper log","x":"Date Paper Problem → Idea → Result My doubt / question"},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#session-log-newest-first","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"Session log (newest first)","x":"<!-- Copy this block for each session:"},{"u":"deep-learning/progress.html#yyyy-mm-dd-module-nn-x-h","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Progress log","h":"YYYY-MM-DD · module NN · X h","x":"- Did: - Clicked: - Still fuzzy: (also add to the Fuzzy list above) - Next: --"},{"u":"deep-learning/interactive/gradient-descent.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Interactive tool","h":"Optimizer Landscape","x":"Modules 01 · 05 · Optimization Race SGD, Momentum, and Adam on valleys, saddles, and bumpy surfaces. See the 2/λ_max stability limit."},{"u":"deep-learning/interactive/mlp-playground.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Interactive tool","h":"Neural Net Playground","x":"Modules 03–05 · Neural nets Train an MLP live: decision boundaries, depth vs width, overfitting, vanishing gradients."},{"u":"deep-learning/interactive/attention.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Interactive tool","h":"Attention & Sampling","x":"Modules 08–09 · Transformers Drag q and k vectors, see why √d matters, the causal mask, and temperature/top-k/top-p."},{"u":"deep-learning/interactive/flashcards.html","p":"🧠 Deep Learning · Interactive tool","h":"Flashcards","x":"All modules · Recall ~50 core facts from math to RL, with spaced repetition."},{"u":"index.html#how-to-split-your-time-senior-year","p":"🏠 Home · Study plan","h":"How to split your time (senior year)","x":"The olympiads and IELTS have hard deadlines this year. Deep learning doesn't, so it can wait or run slowly in the background. 1. Pick ONE primary olympiad (the one where you're strongest or closest to the national team) and give it ~60% of your study time. Medal-level preparation for all three at once rarely works. Keep a second olympiad at maintenance level if its selection rounds are worth entering. 2. IELTS: ~5–7 h/week for 8–10 weeks before your test date. Take the test early enough for one retake before your application deadlines. 3. Deep Learning: ~2–3 h/week of fun (modules 01–04), then"},{"u":"index.html#how-this-repo-cuts-learning-overhead","p":"🏠 Home · Study plan","h":"How this repo cuts learning overhead","x":"1. Skip tests first. Every module begins with a Skip test. If you can do it all, skip the module. 2. One main resource per idea (⭐). Everything else is optional. 3. Recap, don't reread. Skim the Core ideas + cheatsheet.md and drill the flashcards (spaced repetition). 4. See it, then build it. Use the interactive tools for intuition. A module is done once its Build works. 5. Log as you go. Write 3–5 lines in progress.md after each session."},{"u":"guide.html","p":"🧭 Guide","h":"How to use this site","x":"open offline index.html progress export import search keyboard shortcuts"}];